export const STATIC_BODIES = {
 "/tutorials": "<main><h2>What You Will Learn</h2><ul><li>How to calculate CGPA in Nigerian universities</li><li>Understanding WAEC grading system</li><li>JAMB score calculation and admission tips</li><li>Effective study techniques</li></ul><p>Explore a wide range of educational tutorials designed for Nigerian students. Learn how to calculate CGPA, understand WAEC grading system, improve your JAMB score, and discover effective study strategies to excel academically.</p><p>Use our CGPA Calculator, WAEC Calculator, and JAMB Calculator alongside these tutorials.</p></main>",
 "/cgpa-calculator": "<main><h1>Advanced CGPA Calculator</h1><p>Calculate your university CGPA using Nigerian grading system</p><h3>Total Units</h3><h3>Your CGPA</h3><h2>What is CGPA?</h2><p>CGPA stands for Cumulative Grade Point Average. It is the figure Nigerian universities use to summarize a student's overall academic performance across every semester completed so far, from 100 level up to final year. Unlike a single semester's GPA, which resets each term, your CGPA carries forward and updates every time new results are released — it's the number that ends up printed on your transcript and certificate.</p><h2>GPA vs CGPA — what's the difference?</h2><p>GPA (Grade Point Average) covers one semester only. CGPA (Cumulative Grade Point Average) is the running average across all the semesters you've completed. A bad GPA in one semester will pull your CGPA down, but because CGPA is cumulative, a single weak semester matters less as more semesters — and more total credit units — are added to the average.</p><h2>How CGPA is calculated in Nigeria</h2><p>Most Nigerian universities calculate CGPA using this formula:</p><p>CGPA = (Sum of Grade Point × Credit Unit for every course) ÷ (Sum of all Credit Units)</p><p>In practice: for each course, multiply the credit unit (e.g. 2, 3, or 4 units) by the grade point earned (A=5, B=4, C=3, D=2, E=1, F=0). Add up those totals for every course you've ever taken, then divide by the total number of credit units. That single number is your CGPA. This calculator performs that exact calculation for you — just enter each course's unit and grade above.</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>Say a student took four courses in a semester:</p><ul><li>MTH101 (3 units) — grade A (5 points) → 15 grade points</li><li>PHY101 (3 units) — grade B (4 points) → 12 grade points</li><li>CHM101 (2 units) — grade C (3 points) → 6 grade points</li><li>GST101 (2 units) — grade B (4 points) → 8 grade points</li></ul><p>Total grade points = 15 + 12 + 6 + 8 = 41. Total units = 3 + 3 + 2 + 2 = 10. GPA for that semester = 41 ÷ 10 = 4.10. To get your CGPA, you carry this same running total (grade points and units) into the next semester instead of starting over.</p><h2>The Nigerian university grading scale</h2><p>This is the 5-point scale used by the calculator above, and by most (though not all) Nigerian universities:</p><ul><li>A = 5 points (70–100, Excellent)</li><li>B = 4 points (60–69, Very Good)</li><li>C = 3 points (50–59, Good)</li><li>D = 2 points (45–49, Fair)</li><li>E = 1 point (40–44, Pass)</li><li>F = 0 points (Below 40, Fail)</li></ul><p>Note: exact score-to-grade boundaries and the number of points per grade can differ slightly between universities, and some polytechnics and colleges of education use a 4-point scale instead of this 5-point one. Always confirm the exact scale in your own institution's academic handbook.</p><h2>How your CGPA maps to a degree class</h2><p>At graduation, your final CGPA is converted into a degree classification. Use our GPA Class Calculator to see exactly which class your CGPA falls into, along with the typical CGPA ranges for First Class, Second Class Upper, Second Class Lower, Third Class, and Pass.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Explore more tools: You can also check our WAEC Calculator and JAMB Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/waec-grade-calculator": "<main><h1>WAEC Grade Calculator Nigeria</h1><p>Convert WAEC scores into official grades</p><h2>Results</h2><h2>What is the WAEC grading system?</h2><p>WAEC (West African Examinations Council) conducts the WASSCE (West African Senior School Certificate Examination), the exam most Nigerian students sit at the end of senior secondary school. For every subject, your raw score out of 100 is converted into a letter grade from A1 (Excellent) down to F9 (Fail). Universities, polytechnics, and colleges of education use these grades — not the raw scores — to decide admission eligibility.</p><h2>WAEC grade breakdown</h2><ul><li>A1: 75 – 100 (Excellent)</li><li>B2: 70 – 74 (Very Good)</li><li>B3: 65 – 69 (Good)</li><li>C4: 60 – 64 (Credit)</li><li>C5: 55 – 59 (Credit)</li><li>C6: 50 – 54 (Credit)</li><li>D7: 45 – 49 (Pass)</li><li>E8: 40 – 44 (Pass)</li><li>F9: 0 – 39 (Fail)</li></ul><h2>Why the \"credit\" grades matter most</h2><p>Grades A1 through C6 are all classified as credit passes. This distinction matters because most Nigerian tertiary institutions require a minimum number of credit passes — commonly five subjects, almost always including English Language and Mathematics — before you're even eligible to apply for admission, regardless of your JAMB score. D7 and E8 are passes, but they don't count as credits. The exact number of credits and which other subjects must be among them is set by each institution and course, so always confirm the specific requirement for the course you're applying to.</p><h2>How to use this calculator</h2><p>Enter each subject name and the raw score you have (or expect) for it, then press Calculate Grades. The tool converts every score into its WAEC letter grade instantly using the scale above, so you can quickly see which of your results are credit passes.</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>A student scores 78 in Mathematics, 62 in English Language, and 51 in Biology. Using the scale above: 78 converts to A1, 62 converts to C4, and 51 converts to C6. All three are credit passes.</p><h2>WAEC vs NECO</h2><p>NECO (National Examinations Council) is Nigeria's other major school-leaving exam body, and it uses a very similar A1–F9 grading scale. Most institutions treat WAEC and NECO credit passes as equivalent, but always check your target institution's specific admission requirements.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>You can also calculate your CGPA or check your JAMB Score.</p></main>",
 "/jamb-score-calculator": "<main><h1>JAMB Score Calculator Nigeria</h1><p>Enter your UTME subject scores to calculate total</p><h2>What is a JAMB score?</h2><p>JAMB (Joint Admissions and Matriculation Board) administers the UTME (Unified Tertiary Matriculation Examination) — the entrance exam every Nigerian student sits before applying to universities, polytechnics, and colleges of education. Your \"JAMB score\" is the total of your marks across four subjects, out of a maximum possible 400.</p><h2>How the JAMB score is calculated</h2><p>Every candidate sits English Language (compulsory for all courses) plus three other subjects relevant to the course they're applying for — for example, a Physics/Chemistry/Biology combination for Medicine, or Government/Economics/Literature for Mass Communication. Each subject is scored out of 100, and the four scores are simply added together:</p><p>JAMB Total = English score + Subject 2 score + Subject 3 score + Subject 4 score</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>If you score 70 in English, 65 in Mathematics, 60 in Physics, and 55 in Chemistry:</p><p>Total = 70 + 65 + 60 + 55 = 250</p><h2>What counts as a good JAMB score?</h2><p>There's no single official \"good score\" — it depends on your course and target institution's cut-off mark for that admission cycle. The result label this calculator shows you (from \"Excellent\" down to \"Low Admission Chances\") is based on these informal bands, not an official JAMB publication:</p><ul><li>300+ → Excellent, competitive for most courses</li><li>250–299 → Very competitive for most courses</li><li>200–249 → Reasonable for many courses</li><li>180–199 → Around the general minimum range for some institutions</li><li>Below 180 → Likely below JAMB's general minimum for most courses</li></ul><p>Always confirm your target course's actual departmental cut-off mark with the institution, and JAMB's official minimum for the current admission cycle on jamb.gov.ng — both can sit well above (or below) these general ranges, and both change every cycle.</p><h2>After JAMB: what else affects admission?</h2><p>Your UTME score alone rarely decides admission outright. Most institutions also run a Post-UTME screening exercise, and some factor in your WAEC/NECO O'Level grades alongside your JAMB score before making a final offer.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>You can also calculate your CGPA or check the WAEC grading system.</p></main>",
 "/gpa-class-calculator": "<main><h1>GPA Class Calculator Nigeria</h1><p>Enter your CGPA to know your degree classification</p><h3>Nigerian Degree Classification</h3><p>This is the most common scale on Nigerian universities' 5-point system — some institutions use 4.00, not 4.50, as the First Class boundary. See \"A note on variation between institutions\" further down this page.</p><h2>What is degree classification in Nigeria?</h2><p>Degree classification is the honours label — First Class, Second Class Upper, and so on — printed alongside your final CGPA on your degree certificate when you graduate from a Nigerian university. It's a single-tier summary of everything your CGPA already represents: your cumulative academic performance across every semester of your program.</p><h2>How CGPA converts to degree class</h2><p>Most Nigerian universities use the 5-point CGPA scale shown in the table above to place graduating students into one of five classes. Your CGPA is calculated the same way throughout your program — see our CGPA Calculator for the full formula — and at graduation, your final cumulative figure is simply matched against the class ranges above.</p><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>A final CGPA of 4.70 falls in the 4.50–5.00 range, so that graduate receives a First Class degree. A final CGPA of 3.80 falls in the 3.50–4.49 range, so that graduate receives a Second Class Upper. A final CGPA of 2.90 falls in the 2.40–3.49 range — Second Class Lower.</p><h2>Why degree classification matters</h2><ul><li>Many employers use it as an initial screening criterion for graduate roles</li><li>Most postgraduate programs (Master's, PGD) set a minimum class or CGPA for admission</li><li>Some scholarships, fellowships, and NYSC-linked opportunities favour First Class and Second Class Upper graduates</li></ul><h2>A note on variation between institutions</h2><p>The ranges above reflect the most common convention on a 5-point scale, but they are not a single fixed national law — each university's senate sets its own exact boundaries, and a small number of institutions use a 4-point scale with different cut-offs entirely. If you're close to a class boundary, confirm the precise figures in your own institution's academic regulations.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>You can also calculate your CGPA or check your JAMB Score.</p></main>",
 "/age-calculator": "<main><h1>Age Calculator</h1><p>Enter a date of birth to get the exact age in years, months and days, plus total days lived and a countdown to the next birthday.</p><h2>Check an age limit</h2><p>Find out if you meet the minimum or maximum age for a scholarship, admission, job or competition on its cutoff date. Fill in at least one of the two limits.</p><h2>Track many birthdays at once</h2><p>Keep a list of family, friends or classmates and see everyone's age and upcoming birthdays in one place with the Age Tracker.</p><h2>How this age calculator works</h2><p>Your exact age is counted the same way you would do it on paper. First the calculator counts the whole years between your date of birth and the chosen date. Next it counts the whole months since your last birthday. Whatever is left over is the number of days. The totals in months, weeks, days and hours are worked out from the same two dates, and every leap year in between is included automatically.</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>Suppose a student was born on Monday, 15 August 2005, and wants to know their age on 26 October 2026.</p><ul><li>Whole years: from 15 August 2005 to 15 August 2026 is 21 years.</li><li>Whole months: from 15 August 2026 to 15 October 2026 is 2 months.</li><li>Remaining days: from 15 October 2026 to 26 October 2026 is 11 days.</li></ul><p>So the student is 21 years, 2 months and 11 days old. That is 7,742 days in total, which is exactly 1,106 weeks, and their next birthday is 293 days away on 15 August 2027.</p><h2>Uses for an exact age</h2><ul><li>Checking the age requirement on a scholarship, fellowship or competition form.</li><li>Confirming the age you will be on an admission or registration cutoff date.</li><li>Filling in forms that ask for age in years and months, such as school records.</li><li>Planning ahead for a milestone birthday or counting down to the next one.</li></ul><h2>Age limits Nigerian students often meet</h2><p>Many opportunities set an age limit and measure it on a fixed date, not on the day you apply. Scholarships and graduate programmes often have a maximum age, while school and university admission have a minimum age. For NYSC, graduates who are over 30 years old at graduation are exempted from service and receive an exemption certificate instead. Age rules change from time to time, so always confirm the current figure with the organisation, then use the age limit checker above with their exact cutoff date.</p><h2>Leap year birthdays</h2><p>A person born on 29 February has a real birthday only in leap years, such as 2024 and 2028. In the other years this calculator places the birthday on 28 February. Day counts are never affected, because every real calendar day between the two dates is counted.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>More free tools: the Age Tracker, the CGPA Calculator and the JAMB Score Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/age-tracker": "<main><h1>Age Tracker</h1><p>Save the birthdays of family, friends or students and always see their exact age, who has a birthday coming up and the next milestone.</p><h2>Birthdays in the next 30 days</h2><h2>Backup and calendar</h2><p>Your list is saved only in this browser on this device. Export a backup to move it to another phone or computer, or add every birthday to Google Calendar, Apple Calendar or Outlook as a yearly event.</p><h2>What the age tracker does</h2><p>The age tracker keeps a list of people and their dates of birth and works out everything else for you each time you open it. For every person you see their exact age in years, months and days, how many days they have lived, how many days remain until their next birthday and how old they will turn. The list can be sorted by next birthday, name or age, and searched by name or group.</p><h2>Your data stays on your device</h2><p>There is no sign-up. Names and birthdays are stored only in your own browser, using its local storage, and are never sent to our server. The flip side is that clearing your browser data removes the list, and it does not sync between devices by itself. Use the Export backup button to save a copy, then Import backup on another device to bring the same list across.</p><h2>Who it is useful for</h2><ul><li>Class representatives and course reps keeping track of classmates' birthdays.</li><li>Teachers and school administrators who need pupils' ages for records or class placement.</li><li>Parents tracking the ages of their children for school entry and exams.</li><li>Anyone who wants a private birthday reminder list for family and friends.</li></ul><h2>Milestones and the live age counter</h2><p>Besides birthdays, the tracker shows each person's next day-count milestone, such as 8,000 or 10,000 days old, which makes a fun reason to celebrate. Press Live age on anyone to watch their age tick upward in years and seconds. Because only the date of birth is saved, the live count starts from midnight on the day they were born.</p><h2>Birthday calendar</h2><p>The Download birthday calendar button creates a standard calendar file with one yearly event per person. Open it with Google Calendar, Apple Calendar or Outlook and every birthday repeats automatically each year, with reminders handled by your calendar app. People born on 29 February get their event on the last day of February in common years.</p><p>Need a single age instead? Use the Age Calculator to find an exact age on any date or check an age limit. You can also plan exams with the Study Planner.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools": "<main><h1>Engineering Tools</h1><p>Free electronics calculators for engineering and physics students, hobbyists and technicians. Identify components and work out circuit values in seconds.</p><h2>Identify electronic components</h2><p>Most small components do not have their value printed in full. Through-hole resistors use coloured bands, surface-mount resistors use short number and letter codes, and ceramic capacitors use a three-digit code in picofarads. The Resistor Colour Code Calculator, the SMD Resistor Code Calculator and the Capacitor Code Calculator turn these markings into values, and also work the other way round when you need to find a part.</p><h2>Design and analyse circuits</h2><p>Once you know your parts, the circuit calculators do the arithmetic. Use the Ohm's Law Calculator for voltage, current, resistance and power, the Series and Parallel Calculator to combine components, the Voltage Divider Calculator for reference voltages, the LED Resistor Calculator to protect LEDs, and the 555 Timer Calculator for oscillators and delays.</p><h2>Solar, inverter and battery sizing</h2><p>Planning backup power for a home, office or hostel? List your appliances once and the Solar System Calculator sizes the inverter, batteries, panels and charge controller for your city's sunshine. The Inverter Size Calculator and the Battery Backup Calculator answer the two questions people ask most: what size of inverter do I need, and how long will my batteries last?</p><h2>Built for students</h2><p>Every tool explains the formula it uses and includes worked examples, so you can check your own working for practicals, assignments and exams in physics and electrical or electronic engineering. Values can be typed the way engineers write them, such as 4k7 for 4.7 kΩ, 100n for 100 nF or 20mA for 0.02 A, and results use standard component values from the E12, E24 and E96 series where it matters.</p><ul><li>Colour codes follow IEC 60062 and preferred values follow IEC 60063.</li><li>Everything runs in your browser, so the tools work even on a slow connection once the page has loaded.</li><li>All tools are free, with no sign-up.</li></ul></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/resistor-color-code": "<main><h1>Resistor Colour Code Calculator</h1><p>Tap the colours on your resistor to see its value, tolerance and range, or type a value to find the colours. Works for 3, 4, 5 and 6-band resistors and for colour-coded inductors.</p><p>Grey is ±0.01% under IEC 60062:2016; some older parts use grey for ±0.05%.</p><p>A single black band marks a zero-ohm link (a jumper).</p><h2>Find the colours for a value</h2><p>Note: this is not a standard E-series value, so it may be hard to buy.</p><h2>How to read a resistor colour code</h2><p>Resistors are too small to print numbers on, so their value is shown with coloured bands. Each colour stands for a number from 0 to 9. Hold the resistor so the bands that are bunched together are on the left. The band standing on its own on the right, usually gold or silver, is the tolerance band.</p><ul><li>Read the first bands as digits. A 4-band resistor has two digit bands and a 5-band resistor has three.</li><li>The next band is the multiplier: the number of zeros to add, or gold for divide by 10 and silver for divide by 100.</li><li>The last band is the tolerance: how far the real value may be from the marked value.</li><li>On a 6-band resistor the extra band at the end is the temperature coefficient in ppm per kelvin.</li></ul><h2>Resistor colour code chart</h2><p>Values follow the international standard IEC 60062:2016. A popular way to remember the digit order is the phrase BB ROY of Great Britain had a Very Good Wife: Black, Brown, Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Blue, Violet, Grey, White.</p><h2>Worked examples</h2><h3>4-band: yellow, violet, orange, gold</h3><p>Yellow is 4 and violet is 7, so the digits are 47. Orange is a multiplier of 1,000, giving 47,000 Ω or 47 kΩ. Gold means ±5%, so the real resistance lies between 44.65 kΩ and 49.35 kΩ.</p><h3>5-band: brown, black, black, brown, brown</h3><p>The three digits are 1, 0 and 0, which is 100. The brown multiplier is ×10, giving 1,000 Ω or 1 kΩ. The final brown band is a tolerance of ±1%, so the value is between 990 Ω and 1,010 Ω.</p><h3>3-band: red, red, brown</h3><p>The digits are 22 and brown multiplies by 10, so the value is 220 Ω. With no tolerance band the tolerance is ±20%.</p><h2>Standard resistor values (E-series)</h2><p>Resistors are made in preferred values called the E-series, defined in IEC 60063. The E12 series has 12 values in each decade (10, 12, 15, 18, 22, 27, 33, 39, 47, 56, 68, 82) and suits ±10% parts, E24 suits ±5% parts and E96 suits ±1% parts. This calculator tells you when a reading is not a standard value, which usually means a colour was misread or the resistor was read from the wrong end.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>More tools: SMD Resistor Code, Ohm's Law Calculator and Series and Parallel Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/smd-resistor-code": "<main><h1>SMD Resistor Code Calculator</h1><p>Type the marking printed on a surface-mount resistor to get its value. Handles 3-digit, 4-digit, R-notation and EIA-96 codes.</p><p>This marking can be read more than one way. A trailing R is the decimal point on ordinary parts but a ×0.01 multiplier on 1% EIA-96 parts. Measure the resistor with a multimeter, or check the size of the part (EIA-96 codes are used on 1% parts).</p><p>Tolerances shown are the usual ones for this marking system; the code itself does not state the tolerance.</p><h2>Find the SMD code for a value</h2><h2>EIA-96 code table</h2><h2>How SMD resistor codes work</h2><p>Surface-mount (SMD) resistors are too small for colour bands, so their value is printed as a short code. Very small parts, such as the 0402 size and smaller, often have no marking at all and must be measured. There are four common systems.</p><h3>3-digit code</h3><p>Used on standard parts, usually ±5%. The first two digits are the significant figures and the third digit is the number of zeros. For example, 472 is 47 followed by two zeros, which is 4,700 Ω or 4.7 kΩ. A code of 100 is 10 Ω, not 100 Ω.</p><h3>4-digit code</h3><p>Used on precision parts, usually ±1%. The first three digits are the significant figures and the fourth is the number of zeros. For example, 1002 is 100 followed by two zeros, which is 10,000 Ω or 10 kΩ, and 4751 is 4,750 Ω.</p><h3>R notation for small values</h3><p>Values below 10 Ω cannot be written with the digit codes, so the letter R is used as the decimal point. 4R7 is 4.7 Ω, R10 is 0.1 Ω and 0R22 is 0.22 Ω. Current-sense resistors often use this marking.</p><h3>EIA-96 code</h3><p>Small 1% resistors use a three-character code: two digits and a letter. The digits are a position in the E96 list of preferred values (01 = 100, 02 = 102 and so on up to 96 = 976), and the letter is the multiplier. For example, 68X is 499 × 0.1 = 49.9 Ω and 01C is 100 × 100 = 10 kΩ. Use the table above to look up any code.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Reading through-hole parts instead? Use the Resistor Colour Code Calculator or the Capacitor Code Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/capacitor-code": "<main><h1>Capacitor Code Calculator</h1><p>Enter the code printed on a ceramic, film or tantalum capacitor to get its value in pF, nF and µF, plus its tolerance and voltage rating.</p><p>A 9 in the third place means ×0.1 (for values under 10 pF).</p><p>An 8 in the third place means ×0.01.</p><h2>Find the code for a value</h2><p>This value can't be written as a 3-digit code. Codes have two significant figures and run from about 1 pF to 99 µF; larger capacitors (electrolytics) print the value directly.</p><h2>Common capacitor codes</h2><h2>How to read capacitor codes</h2><p>Small ceramic and film capacitors print a three-digit code instead of their full value. The code is always in picofarads (pF). The first two digits are the significant figures and the third digit is the number of zeros to add.</p><ul><li>104 is 10 followed by four zeros: 100,000 pF, which is 100 nF or 0.1 µF.</li><li>222 is 22 followed by two zeros: 2,200 pF, which is 2.2 nF.</li><li>470 is 47 with no zeros: 47 pF. A code of 47 on its own also means 47 pF.</li><li>For values under 10 pF the letter R marks the decimal point, so 4R7 is 4.7 pF.</li></ul><p>European-style markings put the unit letter where the decimal point would be. 2n2 is 2.2 nF, n47 is 0.47 nF (470 pF), 4u7 is 4.7 µF and p68 is 0.68 pF. Large electrolytic capacitors simply print the value and voltage, for example 470 µF 25 V, and must be connected the right way round.</p><h2>Tolerance letters</h2><p>Letters B, C and D give an absolute tolerance in picofarads and are used on small capacitors below about 10 pF.</p><h2>Voltage codes</h2><p>Some capacitors carry an EIA voltage code made of a digit and a letter. Common ones are 0J for 6.3 V, 1A for 10 V, 1C for 16 V, 1E for 25 V, 1V for 35 V, 1H for 50 V, 2A for 100 V, 2E for 250 V and 2J for 630 V. Always choose a capacitor rated comfortably above the highest voltage in your circuit.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Combining capacitors? Use the Series and Parallel Calculator, or time a circuit with the 555 Timer Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/ohms-law-calculator": "<main><h1>Ohm's Law Calculator</h1><p>Fill in any two of voltage, current, resistance and power. The other two are calculated instantly.</p><h2>Ohm's law formulas</h2><p>Ohm's law links voltage (V), current (I) and resistance (R): V = I × R. Electrical power (P) is the rate energy is used: P = V × I. Putting the two together gives every formula you need from any two known values.</p><ul><li>Voltage: V = I × R, V = P ÷ I, or V = √(P × R)</li><li>Current: I = V ÷ R, I = P ÷ V, or I = √(P ÷ R)</li><li>Resistance: R = V ÷ I, R = V² ÷ P, or R = P ÷ I²</li><li>Power: P = V × I, P = I² × R, or P = V² ÷ R</li></ul><h2>Worked examples</h2><h3>Current through a resistor</h3><p>A 220 Ω resistor is connected across a 12 V supply. The current is I = V ÷ R = 12 ÷ 220 = 0.0545 A, or about 54.5 mA. The power is P = V² ÷ R = 144 ÷ 220 = 0.655 W, so a 1 W resistor is needed; a common 0.25 W resistor would overheat.</p><h3>Resistance of a heating element</h3><p>A 1,000 W electric kettle runs on 230 V mains. Its current is I = P ÷ V = 1000 ÷ 230 = 4.35 A, and its element resistance is R = V² ÷ P = 52,900 ÷ 1000 = 52.9 Ω.</p><h2>Units and prefixes</h2><p>Voltage is measured in volts, current in amperes (amps), resistance in ohms and power in watts. In electronics small currents are given in milliamps (1 mA = 0.001 A) and large resistances in kilohms (1 kΩ = 1,000 Ω) or megohms (1 MΩ = 1,000,000 Ω). You can type these prefixes directly into the calculator.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Related tools: Voltage Divider Calculator, LED Resistor Calculator and Resistor Colour Code Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/series-parallel-calculator": "<main><h1>Series and Parallel Circuit Calculator</h1><p>Paste a series or parallel circuit question to get the full working, or combine any number of resistors, capacitors or inductors.</p><h2>Quick total for any components</h2><p>Assumes the inductors are not magnetically coupled (no shared magnetic field).</p><h2>Resistors in series and parallel</h2><p>In a series circuit the same current flows through every resistor, so their resistances simply add: R total = R1 + R2 + R3. In a parallel circuit every resistor has the same voltage across it and the current splits, so the reciprocals add: 1/R total = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3.</p><p>For exactly two resistors in parallel there is a shortcut, product over sum: R = (R1 × R2) ÷ (R1 + R2). For example 100 Ω and 220 Ω in parallel give 22,000 ÷ 320 = 68.75 Ω, and in series they give 320 Ω.</p><h2>Worked example: resistors in series</h2><p>A 24 V supply is connected to 4 Ω and 8 Ω resistors in series. Find the current and the voltage across each resistor.</p><ul><li>Total R = 4 + 8 = 12 Ω</li><li>V across 4 Ω = 2 × 4 = 8 V</li><li>V across 8 Ω = 2 × 8 = 16 V (check: 8 + 16 = 24 V)</li></ul><p>In series the current is the same everywhere and the supply voltage is shared, so the voltages across the resistors always add up to the supply voltage.</p><h2>Worked example: resistors in parallel</h2><p>A 12 V supply is connected to 6 Ω and 3 Ω resistors in parallel. Find the total current.</p><ul><li>Current through 6 Ω = 12 ÷ 6 = 2 A and through 3 Ω = 12 ÷ 3 = 4 A (check: 2 + 4 = 6 A)</li></ul><p>In parallel every resistor has the full supply voltage across it and the currents through the branches add up to the total current.</p><h2>Cells with internal resistance</h2><p>A real cell has some resistance of its own, called its internal resistance r. The current is I = E ÷ (R + r), where E is the emf. The voltage across the external resistors, the terminal p.d., is V = I × R, and the difference E − V = I × r is called the lost volts. For a 12 V cell with r = 1 Ω driving 11 Ω, I = 12 ÷ 12 = 1 A, the terminal p.d. is 11 V and 1 V is lost inside the cell.</p><h2>Capacitors in series and parallel</h2><p>Capacitors follow the opposite rules. In parallel their capacitances add: C total = C1 + C2. In series the reciprocals add: 1/C total = 1/C1 + 1/C2, so two 10 µF capacitors in series give 5 µF. Series capacitors share the voltage, which is why they are sometimes used to reach a higher voltage rating.</p><h2>Inductors in series and parallel</h2><p>Inductors that are far enough apart not to affect each other combine like resistors: they add in series and their reciprocals add in parallel. Coupled inductors, such as two windings on one core, need the mutual inductance as well, which this calculator does not include.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Need a single value? Read it with the Resistor Colour Code Calculator or the Capacitor Code Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/voltage-divider-calculator": "<main><h1>Voltage Divider Calculator</h1><p>Work out the output of a two-resistor voltage divider, or choose the resistor that gives the voltage you need.</p><p>Circuit: Vin → R1 → Vout → R2 → ground (0 V).</p><h2>The voltage divider formula</h2><p>A voltage divider (also called a potential divider) is two resistors in series across a supply. The output is taken from the point between them. Because the same current flows through both resistors, the supply voltage is shared in proportion to their resistances: Vout = Vin × R2 ÷ (R1 + R2).</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>With Vin = 12 V, R1 = 10 kΩ and R2 = 4.7 kΩ, the output is 12 × 4,700 ÷ 14,700 = 3.84 V. The current through both resistors is 12 ÷ 14,700 = 0.816 mA, so each resistor dissipates well under a milliwatt.</p><p>To get 5 V from 12 V with R1 = 10 kΩ, rearrange the formula: R2 = Vout × R1 ÷ (Vin − Vout) = 5 × 10,000 ÷ 7 = 7.14 kΩ. The nearest E24 resistor is 7.5 kΩ, which gives 5.14 V.</p><h2>Where voltage dividers are used</h2><ul><li>Scaling a battery or sensor voltage down for a microcontroller's analogue input.</li><li>Setting the bias voltage of a transistor amplifier.</li><li>Reading light-dependent resistors and thermistors, which form one half of the divider.</li><li>Setting the output voltage of adjustable regulators such as the LM317.</li></ul><p>A potentiometer is a voltage divider in a single part: its wiper splits the track into R1 and R2.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Related tools: Ohm's Law Calculator and Series and Parallel Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/led-resistor-calculator": "<main><h1>LED Resistor Calculator</h1><p>Find the current-limiting resistor for your LEDs, the standard value to buy, and the power rating it needs.</p><h2>LED resistor formula</h2><p>The resistor has to drop the voltage that the LEDs do not use. With n LEDs in series, R = (Vs − n × Vf) ÷ I, where Vs is the supply voltage, Vf is the forward voltage of one LED and I is the LED current in amps. The resistor's power is P = I² × R.</p><h2>Worked example: red LED on a 9 V battery</h2><p>A red LED with a forward voltage of 2 V should run at 20 mA. The resistor must drop 9 − 2 = 7 V, so R = 7 ÷ 0.02 = 350 Ω. The next E12 value up is 390 Ω, which gives a current of 7 ÷ 390 = 17.9 mA. The resistor dissipates about 0.126 W, so a 0.5 W resistor gives a safe margin (a 0.25 W part would run close to its limit).</p><h2>Typical LED forward voltages</h2><ul><li>Red and orange: about 1.8 to 2.2 V</li><li>Yellow and standard green: about 2.0 to 2.4 V</li><li>Blue, white and bright green: about 2.8 to 3.4 V</li><li>Infrared: about 1.2 to 1.5 V</li></ul><p>These are typical figures. The exact forward voltage depends on the LED and the current, so use the datasheet value when you have it.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Related tools: Ohm's Law Calculator and Resistor Colour Code Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/555-timer-calculator": "<main><h1>555 Timer Calculator</h1><p>Calculate the frequency and duty cycle of a 555 astable oscillator, design one for a target frequency, or find a monostable pulse width.</p><p>If the resistors fall outside 1 kΩ – 1 MΩ, choose a different capacitor and calculate again.</p><p>t = 1.1 × R × C. The output goes high for this long after each trigger.</p><h2>How the 555 timer works</h2><p>The 555 is one of the most popular integrated circuits ever made. Inside it are two comparators, a flip-flop and a discharge transistor. A timing capacitor charges through resistors, and the comparators switch the output when the capacitor voltage reaches two-thirds and one-third of the supply voltage. Because both thresholds are fractions of the supply, the timing hardly changes when the supply voltage changes.</p><h2>Astable mode formulas</h2><p>In astable mode the 555 runs continuously and produces a square wave. The capacitor charges through R1 and R2 and discharges through R2 only.</p><ul><li>Time high: t high = 0.693 × (R1 + R2) × C</li><li>Time low: t low = 0.693 × R2 × C</li><li>Frequency: f = 1 ÷ (0.693 × (R1 + 2R2) × C), about 1.44 ÷ ((R1 + 2R2) × C)</li><li>Duty cycle: (R1 + R2) ÷ (R1 + 2R2) × 100%</li></ul><p>Example: R1 = 1 kΩ, R2 = 10 kΩ and C = 1 µF give a frequency of about 68.7 Hz and a duty cycle of 52.4%. The number 0.693 is the natural logarithm of 2, which this calculator uses exactly.</p><h2>Monostable mode</h2><p>In monostable mode the 555 waits for a trigger pulse, then holds its output high for a fixed time t = 1.1 × R × C before returning low. It is used for timers, switch debouncing and delay circuits. For example, R = 100 kΩ and C = 10 µF give a pulse of 1.1 seconds.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Read your parts with the Capacitor Code Calculator and the Resistor Colour Code Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/solar-system-calculator": "<main><h1>Solar System Calculator</h1><p>List the appliances you want to power and get the inverter, batteries, solar panels and charge controller you need, sized for your city's sunshine and shown with the full working.</p><h2>How to size a solar system</h2><p>A solar power system has four main parts. The inverter turns battery power into 230 V mains for your appliances, the batteries store energy for the night and for cloudy days, the solar panels recharge the batteries and power the house in the day, and the charge controller manages the charging. Each is sized from a different number: the inverter from the power of everything that runs at the same time, the batteries from the energy used during backup, and the panels from the energy used per day.</p><h2>Worked example: a family flat in Lagos</h2><p>Eight LED bulbs, three standing fans, a 43 inch TV with a decoder, a medium fridge, a laptop, a Wi-Fi router and three phones, with 10 hours of battery backup at night.</p><ul><li>Running load: 609 W, or 909 W for a moment when the fridge compressor starts</li><li>Energy per day: 4,580 Wh (4.58 kWh); the fridge is counted at 40% of the day because its compressor cycles</li><li>Inverter: 609 W ÷ 0.8 power factor = 761 VA, plus 25% headroom = 952 VA, so a 1 kVA pure sine wave inverter</li><li>Backup energy for 10 hours: 3,572 Wh</li><li>Tubular batteries: 3,572 Wh ÷ (50% usable × 90% inverter efficiency) = 7,938 Wh, which is 331 Ah at 24 V, so four 12 V 220 Ah batteries (two in series, two strings)</li><li>Lithium instead: 3,572 Wh ÷ (80% × 90%) = 4,961 Wh, which is 207 Ah at 24 V, so three 25.6 V 100 Ah batteries</li><li>Solar panels for the rainy season in Lagos (3.5 sun hours): 4,580 Wh ÷ (3.5 × 0.75 × 0.85) = 2,053 W, so five 450 W panels (2,250 W)</li><li>Charge controller: 2,250 W ÷ 24 V × 1.25 = 117 A, so a 120 A MPPT controller, or a hybrid inverter with built-in MPPT</li></ul><p>Sized on the yearly average of 4.6 sun hours instead, the same home would need four panels, but it would run short on cloudy days in June to September.</p><h2>Peak sun hours in Nigeria</h2><p>Peak sun hours are the number of hours of full-strength sunshine a panel receives in a day. The north gets the most, about 5.5 to 6 hours a day in Kano, Sokoto and Maiduguri. The south gets less, about 4.2 to 4.9 hours in Lagos, Port Harcourt, Ibadan and Enugu, falling to about 3.2 to 3.9 hours in the rainy season. The calculator uses rounded figures from satellite averages for 25 cities.</p><h2>Why the system voltage matters</h2><p>Small systems run on 12 V, medium ones on 24 V and large ones on 48 V. Doubling the voltage halves the current, so cables can be thinner, charge controllers smaller and less energy is lost as heat. The calculator picks the voltage from both the inverter size and the size of the solar array, and you can change it.</p><h2>Tips for a reliable system</h2><ul><li>Use a pure sine wave inverter if you run a fridge, freezer, fan or any motor.</li><li>Keep heavy short loads such as pressing irons, kettles, microwaves and pumping machines off small inverters, or run them only in the afternoon sun.</li><li>Replace old bulbs with LED and choose inverter fridges and inverter ACs; saving energy is cheaper than adding panels.</li><li>Fit a fuse or DC breaker close to the battery and keep battery cables short.</li><li>Clean the panels every few weeks during the harmattan, when dust can cut output noticeably.</li></ul><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Only need part of the system? Use the Inverter Size Calculator or the Battery Backup Calculator. Your appliance list is shared between all three.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/inverter-size-calculator": "<main><h1>Inverter Size Calculator</h1><p>Find the inverter size in kVA for your appliances, checked against the starting surge of motors, with the battery voltage, fuse and cable size.</p><h2>How to calculate inverter size</h2><ul><li>Add the running watts of every appliance that can be on at the same time.</li><li>Divide by the power factor, usually 0.8, to get volt-amperes (VA).</li><li>Add 20 to 30% headroom so the inverter isn't working at its limit.</li><li>Check the starting surge. Fridges, freezers, ACs and pumps draw two to three times their running power for a moment when the motor starts, and most inverters can only give about twice their rating for a few seconds.</li><li>Round up to the next standard size: 1, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 5, 7.5 or 10 kVA.</li></ul><h2>Worked example</h2><p>Eight LED bulbs, three fans, a 43 inch TV, a decoder, a fridge, a laptop, a router and three phone chargers draw 609 W together.</p><ul><li>761 VA × 1.25 headroom = 952 VA</li><li>When the fridge starts the load jumps to about 909 W for a moment, well within what a 1 kVA inverter can surge to</li><li>Result: a 1 kVA pure sine wave inverter, loaded to 76% of its 800 W rating</li></ul><h2>Battery voltage, fuse and cable</h2><p>The inverter draws its power from the battery bank as direct current. At full output the battery current is the inverter's watts divided by the battery voltage and the inverter efficiency, so an 800 W inverter on 24 V draws about 37 A. The fuse or DC breaker should be about 25% above this, and the cable thick enough for the fuse rating. Higher battery voltages mean lower currents, which is why inverters of 5 kVA and above normally run on 48 V.</p><h2>Pure sine wave or modified sine wave?</h2><p>A pure sine wave inverter produces power like the grid and is safe for every appliance. Modified sine wave inverters are cheaper but make motors, fridges, fans and some chargers run hot and noisy. If you have any motor load, choose pure sine wave.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Next, find out how many batteries you need with the Battery Backup Calculator, or size the whole system, including solar panels, with the Solar System Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/battery-backup-calculator": "<main><h1>Battery Backup Calculator</h1><p>Find how long your inverter batteries will last, or how many batteries you need for the hours of backup you want.</p><h2>How to calculate battery backup time</h2><ul><li>Battery energy in watt-hours = volts × amp-hours × number of batteries.</li><li>Usable energy = battery energy × depth of discharge × inverter efficiency (about 90%).</li><li>Backup time in hours = usable energy ÷ load in watts.</li></ul><p>Two 12 V 200 Ah tubular batteries store 2 × 12 × 200 = 4,800 Wh. Used to 50% through a 90% efficient inverter, that is 2,160 Wh, so a 300 W load runs for about 7.2 hours. The same capacity in lithium, used to 80%, gives about 11.5 hours.</p><h2>How to calculate the number of batteries</h2><ul><li>Work out the energy your appliances use during the backup hours.</li><li>Divide by the depth of discharge and the inverter efficiency to get the energy the bank must store.</li><li>Divide by the system voltage to get the amp-hours needed.</li><li>Put enough batteries in series to reach the system voltage (two 12 V batteries for 24 V, four for 48 V), then add parallel strings until you have enough amp-hours.</li></ul><h2>Battery types</h2><p>Tubular flooded batteries are the most common in Nigeria. They are robust and cheaper, but need distilled water topping and should only be discharged to about 50%. AGM and gel batteries are sealed and need no maintenance but have a shorter life in hot rooms. Lithium iron phosphate (LiFePO4) batteries cost more but give much more usable energy, recharge faster on solar and last two to three times as long.</p><h2>Make your batteries last longer</h2><ul><li>Recharge fully as soon as possible after a long outage; lead-acid batteries left half empty lose capacity.</li><li>Keep the batteries in a cool, ventilated place, away from direct sun.</li><li>Check the water level of tubular batteries every month and top up with distilled water only.</li><li>Set the inverter's charging current and low-voltage cut-off to suit the battery type.</li></ul><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Need the inverter too? Use the Inverter Size Calculator, or size panels, batteries and inverter together with the Solar System Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/aggregate-score-calculator": "<main><h1>Post-UTME Aggregate Score Calculator</h1><p>Work out your admission aggregate from your UTME, post-UTME and O'level grades using your university's formula, and see the score you need for your course.</p><h2>What is an aggregate score?</h2><p>Your aggregate score is the single number a university uses to rank candidates for admission. It combines your UTME (JAMB) score with your post-UTME screening result and, at some universities, your O'level grades. Each department then admits candidates from the top of the list down to its cut-off mark.</p><h2>The four common aggregate formulas</h2><ul><li>UTME and post-UTME, 50 : 50. Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2. Reported for UI, UNIBEN, UNIPORT, ABU and UNICAL.</li><li>UTME, post-UTME and O'level, 50 : 30 : 20. UTME ÷ 8, plus the post-UTME out of 30, plus O'level points out of 20. Reported for UNILAG and UNILORIN.</li><li>UTME, post-UTME and O'level, 50 : 40 : 10. Reported for OAU.</li><li>UTME and O'level only. Schools that screen without a written test, such as LASU (50 : 50) and UNN (60 : 40).</li></ul><h2>How O'level grades become points</h2><p>Schools that count O'level grades share out the O'level marks across your five best relevant subjects. An A1 earns the full share for a subject and each lower grade earns a little less: B2 earns 90%, B3 80%, C4 70%, C5 60% and C6 50% of it. When O'level is worth 20 marks, that is A1 = 4, B2 = 3.6, B3 = 3.2, C4 = 2.8, C5 = 2.4 and C6 = 2. Grades D7, E8 and F9 earn nothing and usually mean you don't meet the requirements.</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>A candidate scores 256 in UTME and 67 in a post-UTME test marked out of 100 at a school using the 50 : 50 formula.</p><ul><li>UTME: 256 ÷ 8 = 32</li><li>Post-UTME: 67 ÷ 2 = 33.5</li><li>Aggregate = 32 + 33.5 = 65.5 out of 100</li></ul><h2>Formulas change, so check yours</h2><p>Universities can change their screening method from one year to the next, and none of them publishes one formula that stays fixed. The formulas here are the ones most widely reported for recent screening exercises. Always compare them with your school's current post-UTME notice, and use \"Change the formula\" if the weights differ.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Want to know your chances? Use the Admission Predictor. At university, keep track of your results with the CGPA Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/cgpa-converter": "<main><h1>CGPA Converter</h1><p>Convert your CGPA from the Nigerian 5-point scale to the 4.0 scale (or back), to a percentage and to a class of degree, for scholarships, jobs and study-abroad applications.</p><h2>How to convert a 5.0 CGPA to the 4.0 scale</h2><p>The standard method is proportional: divide your CGPA by the maximum of your scale and multiply by the maximum of the new one. On a 5-point scale, 4.50 ÷ 5 × 4 = 3.60 on the 4.0 scale, and 3.50 ÷ 5 × 4 = 2.80. Going the other way, 3.20 on a 4-point scale is 3.20 ÷ 4 × 5 = 4.00 on the 5-point scale.</p><h2>CGPA to percentage</h2><p>A simple percentage is CGPA ÷ maximum × 100, so 4.00 out of 5 is 80%. This is a share of the maximum, not your average mark. A Nigerian A is any mark from 70 to 100, so an average mark cannot be recovered from a CGPA. If a form asks for your average score, use your transcript.</p><h2>Course-by-course conversion (WES style)</h2><p>Credential evaluators such as WES change each course grade to a 4.0 grade first, then average them. With the common table below, A becomes 4.0, B becomes 3.0, C becomes 2.0, and D and E become 1.0. Because a B is 4 of 5 on the Nigerian scale but only 3 of 4 on the US scale, this usually gives a lower result than the proportional method for students with many B grades.</p><ul><li>A (70–100): 5 points on the 5-point scale, 4.0 on the 4.0 scale</li><li>B (60–69): 4 points, 3.0</li><li>C (50–59): 3 points, 2.0</li><li>D (45–49): 2 points, 1.0</li><li>E (40–44): 1 point, 1.0</li><li>F (0–39): 0 points, 0.0</li></ul><h2>Class of degree on each scale</h2><p>On the 5-point scale used by most Nigerian universities: First Class 4.50–5.00, Second Class Upper 3.50–4.49, Second Class Lower 2.40–3.49, Third Class 1.50–2.39 and Pass 1.00–1.49.</p><p>On a university 4-point scale: First Class 3.50–4.00, Second Class Upper 3.00–3.49, Second Class Lower 2.00–2.99 and Third Class 1.00–1.99. Polytechnic ND and HND results on the 4-point scale are Distinction 3.50–4.00, Upper Credit 3.00–3.49, Lower Credit 2.50–2.99 and Pass 2.00–2.49.</p><p>Each senate sets its own boundaries, so check your institution's academic regulations if your CGPA is close to a boundary.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Need to work out your CGPA first? Use the CGPA Calculator, then check your result with the Class of Degree Calculator. Looking for funding abroad? See Scholarships for Nigerian Students.</p></main>",
 "/jamb-cbt-practice": "<main><h1>Free JAMB CBT Practice Test</h1><p>Practise for the UTME on a computer-based test that works like the real one: timed questions, a question navigator, and a full review with an explanation for every answer.</p><h2>How the JAMB UTME CBT works</h2><p>The Unified Tertiary Matriculation Examination (UTME) is taken on a computer at an accredited CBT centre. You answer 180 multiple-choice questions in 2 hours: 60 in Use of English and 40 in each of the three other subjects for your course. Each question has four options, and you can move freely between questions and subjects until you submit or the time runs out.</p><p>Each subject is scored out of 100, so your UTME score is out of 400. There is no negative marking, so an unanswered question scores the same as a wrong one: always choose an answer before time runs out.</p><h2>How to use this practice test</h2><ul><li>Pick a subject and choose 10, 20 or 40 questions, or practise a single topic you find difficult.</li><li>Choose Timed test to practise at the UTME pace of 40 seconds a question, or Study mode to see each answer and explanation straight away.</li><li>Use the numbered navigator to jump between questions, and flag any you want to come back to.</li><li>After you submit, check your score by topic, read the explanations, and retry only the questions you got wrong.</li></ul><h2>Why practise with original questions?</h2><p>Past questions are useful, but many students end up remembering answers instead of learning the topic. Every question here is new and written to the JAMB syllabus, and the options are shuffled each time, so a good score shows you really understand the work. Each explanation shows the working or the reason, and points out the mistake behind the most tempting wrong option.</p><h2>A simple plan for the last weeks before the UTME</h2><ul><li>Take one timed 40-question test in each subject to find your starting point.</li><li>Spend most of your study time on the two or three weakest topics shown in your results.</li><li>Use Study mode on those topics until the explanations make sense, then retake a timed test.</li><li>In the final week, practise at full exam pace and get used to finishing with time to spare.</li></ul><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Once you have your UTME result, use the JAMB Score Calculator and the Post-UTME Aggregate Calculator to see where you stand for admission.</p></main>",
 "/study-planner": "<main><h1>Study Planner for Students</h1><p>Your study plans automatically expire after 7 days</p><p>No study tasks yet</p><h2>What is a Study Planner?</h2><p>A study planner helps students organize their daily academic tasks, manage time effectively, and improve productivity.</p><h2>Why You Need a Study Planner</h2><ul><li>Stay organized with your study schedule</li><li>Avoid last-minute cramming</li><li>Improve academic performance</li><li>Manage your time effectively</li><li>Receive automatic study reminders</li></ul><h2>How This Planner Works</h2><p>Add your study tasks with a date and time. StudentToolsNG will remind you using browser notifications and alarm sounds.</p><h2>Important Notice</h2><p>Study tasks automatically expire after 7 days to help students stay active and organized.</p><p>You can also use our CGPA Calculator and JAMB Score Calculator .</p><h2>// Smart Study Planner //</h2><p>// Your study plans automatically expire // after 7 days //</p><p>// No study plans yet //</p><h2>// What is a Study Planner? //</h2><p>// A study planner helps students // organize their academic tasks, // manage time effectively, // and improve productivity. //</p><h2>// Why Use This Planner? //</h2><ul><li>// Improve focus and consistency //</li><li>// Avoid procrastination //</li><li>// Track daily study sessions //</li><li>// Build better study habits //</li><li>// Plans auto-delete after 7 days //</li></ul><h2>// Student Productivity Tips //</h2><p>// Study difficult subjects first, // revise consistently, // and create achievable daily goals. //</p><p>// You can also use our // // CGPA Calculator // // and // // JAMB Score Calculator // //</p><h2>Study Planner for Students</h2><h2>What is a Study Planner?</h2><p>// A study planner helps students organize their daily academic tasks, // manage time effectively, and improve productivity. //</p><h2>Why You Need a Study Planner</h2><ul><li>Stay organized with your subjects</li><li>Avoid last-minute cramming</li><li>Improve academic performance</li><li>Manage your time better</li></ul><h2>How to Use This Study Planner</h2><p>// Add your daily study tasks above and track your progress. You can // delete tasks once completed. //</p><h2>Study Tips for Nigerian Students</h2><p>// Create a daily routine, focus on difficult subjects first, and // use tools like CGPA calculators to track your progress. //</p><p>// You can also use our // CGPA Calculator // and // JAMB Score Calculator. //</p></main>",
 "/scholarships": "<main><h1>Scholarship Opportunities</h1><p>Learn about eligibility, application process, and deadlines for this scholarship.</p><h2>Scholarships for Nigerian Students</h2><p>Scholarships help Nigerian students reduce the cost of education and achieve their academic goals without financial stress. Many organizations including government bodies and private companies offer scholarships yearly.</p><h2>Types of Scholarships Available</h2><ul><li>Undergraduate scholarships</li><li>Postgraduate scholarships</li><li>Merit-based scholarships</li><li>Need-based scholarships</li></ul><h2>How to Apply for Scholarships</h2><p>To apply for scholarships in Nigeria, students should monitor deadlines, prepare required documents, and apply through official portals.</p><h2>Who Offers Scholarships in Nigeria?</h2><ul><li>Federal and state governments: through scholarship boards and education trust funds, often for undergraduates in Nigerian universities and for study abroad in priority courses.</li><li>Companies: especially oil and gas, banking and telecoms firms, many of which run yearly undergraduate awards for students in their host communities or in particular courses.</li><li>Foundations and NGOs: private and religious foundations that support students with financial need or outstanding results.</li><li>Universities: internal awards for the best students in a faculty or department.</li><li>Foreign governments and international bodies: for example the Commonwealth and Chevening scholarships for postgraduate study in the United Kingdom.</li></ul><h2>Common Eligibility Requirements</h2><ul><li>Admission into, or current study at, an accredited institution.</li><li>A minimum academic standard, such as five O-level credits including English and Mathematics, a minimum JAMB score, or a minimum CGPA (often 3.0 or 3.5 on a 5-point scale).</li><li>A particular course of study, level, state of origin or community.</li><li>Proof of financial need for need-based awards.</li><li>For postgraduate and international awards: a good first degree, work experience and evidence of leadership.</li></ul><p>Not sure where you stand? Use our CGPA Calculator and GPA Class Calculator to check your result against the minimum required before you apply.</p><h2>Documents You Usually Need</h2><ul><li>Birth certificate or declaration of age, and a valid means of identification (NIN slip, passport).</li><li>O-level results (WAEC or NECO) and JAMB result slip.</li><li>University admission letter and current student ID card.</li><li>Academic transcript or statement of result showing your CGPA.</li><li>Local government or state of origin certificate, where required.</li><li>Passport photographs.</li><li>A personal statement or essay, and reference letters for many awards.</li></ul><p>Scan these documents clearly in advance so you can apply quickly when a scholarship opens.</p><h2>Tips for a Strong Application</h2><ul><li>Start early: many scholarships open and close within a few weeks each year.</li><li>Read the requirements carefully and submit every document asked for; incomplete applications are often rejected automatically.</li><li>Write your own essay: explain your goals, your challenges, what you have achieved and how the award will help you. Be specific and honest.</li><li>Choose referees who know you well, such as a lecturer or principal, and give them enough time.</li><li>Prepare for aptitude tests and interviews: many company scholarships include an online test in verbal and numerical reasoning.</li><li>Keep your grades up: most awards require you to maintain a minimum CGPA to keep the scholarship.</li></ul><h2>Avoid Scholarship Scams</h2><ul><li>Genuine scholarships do not ask you to pay a fee to receive an award.</li><li>Apply only through the official website or portal of the organisation offering the scholarship.</li><li>Be wary of messages promising guaranteed awards or asking for your bank PIN or BVN.</li><li>Check announcements on the organisation's official website or verified social media pages.</li></ul><p>You can also use our CGPA Calculator to track your academic performance while applying for scholarships.</p></main>",
 "/admission-predictor": "<main><h1>Admission Chance Calculator Nigeria</h1><h2>How Admission is Calculated in Nigeria</h2><p>Most Nigerian universities use a combination of JAMB and Post UTME scores to determine admission eligibility. Each school has its own formula.</p><h2>How This Admission Predictor Works</h2><p>This tool estimates your admission chances using a weighted formula: 60% JAMB score and 40% Post UTME score.</p><h2>Example Calculation</h2><p>If you score 250 in JAMB and 70 in Post UTME:</p><h2>Admission Chance Guide</h2><ul><li>70+ → High Admission Chance</li><li>60–69 → Moderate Chance</li><li>Below 60 → Low Chance</li></ul><h2>Important Note</h2><p>Admission depends on your course, school cut-off mark, and competition level. This tool provides an estimate, not a guarantee.</p><p>You can also calculate your JAMB Score and CGPA.</p></main>",
 "/ai-tutor": "<main><h1>AI Tutor for Students</h1><h3>Answer</h3><h2>What is an AI Tutor?</h2><p>An AI tutor is an intelligent learning assistant that helps students solve problems, understand concepts, and get instant answers to academic questions.</p><h2>How This AI Tutor Helps You</h2><ul><li>Answer academic questions instantly</li><li>Explain difficult topics</li><li>Help with assignments and homework</li><li>Support learning in mathematics, science, and more</li></ul><h2>How to Use the AI Tutor</h2><p>Type your question in the input box above and click \"Ask AI\". The system will generate a helpful answer instantly.</p><h2>Example Questions You Can Ask</h2><p>What is CGPA?</p><p>Solve 2x + 4 = 10</p><p>Explain WAEC grading system</p><p>You can also use our CGPA Calculator, WAEC Calculator, and JAMB Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/tutorials/math-calculator": "<main><h1>Smart Math Calculator</h1><h2>How to Use the Smart Math Calculator</h2><p>Type a maths question in plain words or numbers, then press Solve. The calculator works out the answer and shows the steps, so you can follow the method and use it in your own WAEC, NECO, JAMB or class exercises. You can also tap any of the example chips above the input box to try a question instantly.</p><ul><li>Type the question, for example 2x + 4 = 10 or 20% of 150.</li><li>Use / for fractions (3/4), ^ for powers (2^3) and : for ratios (2:3).</li><li>For two equations, separate them with \"and\" or a comma.</li><li>Read the steps, not just the final answer, so you learn the method.</li></ul><h2>Topics the Calculator Can Solve</h2><ul><li>Algebra: linear equations such as 3x − 5 = 16 and x/2 + 3 = 7.</li><li>Simultaneous equations: pairs such as 2x + y = 5 and x − y = 1.</li><li>Fractions: adding, subtracting, multiplying and dividing.</li><li>Percentages: percentage of a quantity, percentage increase and decrease, reverse percentages.</li><li>Ratio and proportion: sharing a quantity in a given ratio.</li><li>Simple interest: from principal (p), rate (r) and time (t).</li><li>Profit and loss: from cost price and selling price, including profit percent.</li><li>Speed, distance and time.</li><li>Averages, indices, sets (Venn diagram counts), area and perimeter, age and mixture problems.</li><li>Physics: force from mass and acceleration (F = ma).</li></ul><h2>Worked Examples</h2><h3>1. Solve 2x + 4 = 10</h3><p>Subtract 4 from both sides: 2x = 6. Divide both sides by 2: x = 3. Check: 2 × 3 + 4 = 10.</p><h3>2. Add 1/2 + 3/4</h3><p>The lowest common denominator is 4. 1/2 = 2/4, so 2/4 + 3/4 = 5/4 = 1 1/4.</p><h3>3. Divide ₦100 000 in the ratio 2 : 3</h3><p>Total parts = 2 + 3 = 5. One part = 100 000 ÷ 5 = 20 000. Shares: 2 × 20 000 = ₦40 000 and 3 × 20 000 = ₦60 000.</p><h3>4. Simple interest on ₦1000 at 5% for 2 years</h3><p>I = (P × R × T) ÷ 100 = (1000 × 5 × 2) ÷ 100 = ₦100. Amount = 1000 + 100 = ₦1100.</p><h3>5. Solve 2x + y = 5 and x − y = 1</h3><p>Add the equations to eliminate y: 3x = 6, so x = 2. Substitute into x − y = 1: 2 − y = 1, so y = 1. Answer: x = 2, y = 1.</p><h3>6. A trader buys goods for ₦400 and sells them for ₦500</h3><p>Profit = 500 − 400 = ₦100. Profit percent = (100 ÷ 400) × 100 = 25%.</p><h2>Tips for Using a Math Solver Well</h2><ul><li>Try the question yourself first, then use the calculator to check your answer.</li><li>If your answer differs, compare each step to find exactly where the mistake happened.</li><li>In examinations you must show your working, so copy the method, not just the answer.</li><li>Practise similar questions in our practice quiz and read the step-by-step mathematics tutorials.</li></ul></main>",
 "/quiz": "<main><h2>🧠 Quiz Mode</h2><h2>Practice Mathematics with Interactive Quiz</h2><p>This math quiz helps students practice key topics such as algebra, fractions, percentages, ratios, and simple interest. Each question is generated dynamically to improve problem-solving skills and speed.</p><h3>Topics Covered</h3><ul><li>Algebra equations</li><li>Fractions and ratios</li><li>Percentages</li><li>Simple interest</li><li>Set theory basics</li></ul><h3>Why Use This Quiz?</h3><p>Practicing regularly helps students build confidence and improve accuracy in exams like WAEC and JAMB. The leaderboard feature also allows students to compete and track their performance.</p><h3>Related Tools</h3><ul><li>Math Solver</li><li>CGPA Calculator</li><li>JAMB Calculator</li></ul><h3>🏆 What should we call you?</h3><p>Enter a name so your score shows up correctly on the leaderboard — no email or password needed.</p></main>",
 "/marketplace": "<main><h1>Research Library</h1><p>Project materials, research documents and study guides from independent publishers. Read chapters 1 &amp; 2 free on every document — unlock the full work when it's what you need.</p><h2>How the Research Library Works</h2><ul><li>Search or browse by keyword or field of study to find project guides and research documents.</li><li>Read the free preview: chapters 1 and 2 of every document are open to read, so you can judge its quality and relevance before paying.</li><li>Unlock the full document with a secure online payment if it is what you need. It is then available under My Purchases.</li></ul><h2>Using Research Materials Responsibly</h2><p>Documents in the library are guides to help you plan and write your own work: how to frame a research problem, review literature, choose a methodology, design a questionnaire and present results. Use them to learn the structure and approach, collect your own data, and always cite any source you quote or adapt. Submitting someone else's work as your own is plagiarism and can lead to serious academic penalties.</p><h2>Have research or project work to sell?</h2><p>Publish it here, set your price, and get paid straight to your bank account every time someone unlocks it.</p></main>",
 "/about": "<main><h1>About StudentToolsNG</h1><p>StudentToolsNG is an educational platform designed to help students easily calculate their academic results and access helpful learning resources.</p><p>Our platform provides free tools such as CGPA calculators, WAEC grade calculators, and JAMB score calculators to simplify academic planning for Nigerian students.</p><p>In addition to academic tools, StudentToolsNG also provides tutorials, study guides, and scholarship opportunities to help students succeed in school.</p><h2>Our Mission</h2><p>Our mission is to make academic tools and educational resources easily accessible to every Nigerian student.</p><h2>What We Offer</h2><ul><li>CGPA Calculator for university students</li><li>WAEC grade calculator</li><li>JAMB score calculator</li><li>Study tutorials and guides</li><li>Scholarship opportunities</li><li>Study planning tools</li></ul><h2>Contact Us</h2><p>If you have questions, suggestions, or feedback, feel free to reach out through our contact page.</p><h2>Who We Are</h2><p>StudentToolsNG was created to solve real problems faced by Nigerian students when calculating CGPA, understanding WAEC grading, and preparing for JAMB.</p><p>We understand the challenges students face, and we are committed to providing simple, accurate, and reliable academic tools.</p><p>You can explore our CGPA Calculator, WAEC Calculator, and JAMB Calculator to get started.</p><h2>Why Choose StudentToolsNG?</h2><ul><li>100% Free academic tools</li><li>Designed specifically for Nigerian students</li><li>Simple and easy to use</li><li>Accurate and reliable calculations</li></ul></main>",
 "/contact": "<main><h1>Contact Us</h1><h2>Get in Touch with StudentToolsNG</h2><p>Do you have questions about our CGPA calculator, WAEC grading system, or JAMB score tools? We are here to help Nigerian students succeed academically.</p><h2>Why Contact Us?</h2><ul><li>Get help using our academic tools</li><li>Report bugs or issues</li><li>Request new features</li><li>Partnership and collaboration enquiries</li></ul><h2>Support for Nigerian Students</h2><p>StudentToolsNG provides free tools and tutorials designed specifically for Nigerian universities, WAEC, and JAMB students.</p><p>You can also explore our CGPA Calculator, WAEC Calculator, and JAMB Calculator.</p><h2>What to Include in Your Message</h2><ul><li>The page or tool you were using, for example the CGPA calculator or a particular tutorial.</li><li>What you expected to happen and what actually happened.</li><li>The device and browser you used, if you are reporting a problem.</li><li>For a tutorial request, the subject, topic and level (WAEC, JAMB or university).</li></ul><p>Clear details help us answer you faster and fix problems for every student.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>Are the tools on StudentToolsNG free?</h3><p>Yes. The CGPA, WAEC, JAMB and GPA class calculators, the study planner, the math calculator and the tutorials are free to use.</p><h3>How is my CGPA calculated?</h3><p>Each course's grade point is multiplied by its credit units, the results are added together, and the total is divided by the total units. Our CGPA Calculator does this for you and explains how CGPA works.</p><h3>Can I suggest a tutorial topic?</h3><p>Yes. Send us the subject and topic using the form above. We publish new physics, mathematics, biology, chemistry and programming tutorials regularly.</p><h3>Can I sell my research or project materials?</h3><p>Yes. Publishers can list research documents in our Research Library and set their own price.</p><h3>How is my information used?</h3><p>Please read our Privacy Policy for details of the information we collect and how it is used.</p></main>",
 "/privacy-policy": "<main><h1>Privacy Policy</h1><p>This Privacy Policy explains how StudentToolsNG collects, uses, and protects information when you use our website.</p><h2>1. Information We Collect</h2><p>StudentToolsNG may collect limited information such as:</p><ul><li>Name and email submitted through contact forms</li><li>Usage data such as pages visited</li><li>Device and browser information</li></ul><h2>2. How We Use Your Information</h2><p>We use the information collected to:</p><ul><li>Improve our academic tools</li><li>Respond to user inquiries</li><li>Analyze website performance</li><li>Provide better learning resources</li></ul><h2>3. Cookies</h2><p>StudentToolsNG may use cookies to improve user experience, analyze traffic, and remember user preferences.</p><h2>4. Third-Party Services</h2><p>We may use third-party services such as analytics providers and hosting platforms to improve the performance of the website.</p><h2>4a. Advertising (Google AdSense)</h2><p>StudentToolsNG uses Google AdSense to display advertisements. Google, as a third-party vendor, uses cookies to serve ads based on your prior visits to this website or other websites. Google's use of advertising cookies enables it and its partners to serve ads based on your visit to this site and/or other sites on the internet.</p><p>You may opt out of personalized advertising by visiting Google Ads Settings . Alternatively, you can opt out of a third-party vendor's use of cookies for personalized advertising by visiting www.aboutads.info .</p><h2>5. External Links</h2><p>Our website may contain links to external websites. We are not responsible for the privacy practices of those websites.</p><h2>6. Data Security</h2><p>We take reasonable steps to protect user information from unauthorized access or disclosure.</p><h2>7. Children's Information</h2><p>StudentToolsNG is designed for educational purposes. We do not knowingly collect personal data from children without parental consent.</p><h2>8. Changes to This Policy</h2><p>We may update this privacy policy from time to time. Updates will be posted on this page.</p><h2>9. Contact Us</h2><p>If you have questions about this Privacy Policy, please contact us through the contact page.</p><p>Explore more tools: You can also check our WAEC Calculator and JAMB Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/terms": "<main><h1>Terms and Conditions for StudentToolsNG</h1><p>Welcome to StudentToolsNG. By accessing and using this website, you agree to comply with the following terms and conditions.</p><h2>Use of Our Tools</h2><p>StudentToolsNG provides academic tools such as CGPA calculators, WAEC grade calculators, and JAMB score calculators for educational purposes only.</p><p>While we strive to provide accurate calculations, users should verify their academic results with their respective institutions.</p><h2>Educational Content</h2><p>Tutorials and educational materials published on StudentToolsNG are intended to support students in their learning journey.</p><p>We do not guarantee academic outcomes based on the use of our tools or tutorials.</p><h2>User Responsibility</h2><p>Users are responsible for ensuring the accuracy of the information they enter into any calculators or tools on this website.</p><h2>External Links</h2><p>StudentToolsNG may contain links to external websites. We are not responsible for the content or practices of these external sites.</p><h2>Changes to These Terms</h2><p>We reserve the right to update these terms at any time. Users are encouraged to review this page periodically.</p><h2>Contact</h2><p>If you have questions about these Terms and Conditions, please contact us through the contact page.</p><p>You can also use our CGPA Calculator and JAMB Score Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/author": "<main><h1>Engr. Henry Akpan</h1><p>Electrical and Electronics Engineer, Full-Stack Web Developer and Mathematics &amp; Physics Tutor. Founder of StudentToolsNG.</p><p>Passionate about building technology solutions that help students succeed academically.</p><h2>About the Author</h2><p>Engr. Henry Akpan is an Electrical Engineer and Full-Stack Developer with a passion for building educational tools that simplify learning for Nigerian students.</p><p>He is the founder of StudentToolsNG, a platform that provides free academic calculators, tutorials, and study resources.</p><h2>Skills &amp; Expertise</h2><ul><li>Full-Stack Web Development (React, Node.js)</li><li>Electrical and Electronics Engineer</li><li>UI UX Designer</li><li>Mathematics and Physics Tutoring</li><li>Educational Technology Solutions</li></ul><p>Explore tools created by Henry such as the CGPA Calculator, WAEC Calculator, and JAMB Calculator.</p><h2>Articles by Henry Akpan</h2><ul><li>How to Calculate CGPA</li><li>WAEC Grading System</li></ul></main>",
 "/aggregate-score-calculator/unilag": "<main><h1>UNILAG Aggregate Score Calculator</h1><p>UNILAG combines three parts: your UTME score converted to 50 marks, your post-UTME screening test scored out of 30, and your five O'level credits worth up to 20 marks.</p><h2>How UNILAG calculates your aggregate score</h2><p>Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME (out of 30) + O'level points (out of 20). The weighting is UTME 50%, post-UTME 30%, O'level 20%.</p><ul><li>The UTME part is your JAMB score divided by 8, so 400 becomes 50.</li><li>The post-UTME test is reported to be marked out of 30 and added as it is. Candidates who score very low in the test are reported to be disqualified whatever their UTME score.</li><li>O'level points are A1 = 4, B2 = 3.6, B3 = 3.2, C4 = 2.8, C5 = 2.4 and C6 = 2 for five subjects, including English Language and Mathematics.</li><li>UNILAG has asked for a UTME score of at least 200 with UNILAG as first choice before you can apply for screening.</li></ul><h2>UNILAG aggregate score worked example</h2><p>A candidate with 280 in UTME and 22 in the post-UTME test (out of 30) and O'level grades A1, B2, B3, C4, C6 scores:</p><ul><li>UTME: 280 ÷ 8 = 35 (out of 50)</li><li>Post-UTME: 22 out of 30 = 22</li><li>O'level: A1 = 4, B2 = 3.6, B3 = 3.2, C4 = 2.8, C6 = 2 → total 15.6 (out of 20; A1 = 4 per subject)</li><li>Aggregate = 35 + 22 + 15.6 = 72.6 out of 100</li></ul><h2>What aggregate score do I need for UNILAG?</h2><p>There is no single pass mark. After screening, UNILAG publishes departmental cut-off marks for each course, and they change from year to year with the number of applicants. Competitive courses such as Medicine, Law, Nursing and Pharmacy always need a much higher aggregate than the general cut-off.</p><p>Enter last year's cut-off for your course as the target in the calculator to see the post-UTME score you would need to reach it.</p><h2>Before you rely on the result</h2><p>The University of Lagos does not publish one fixed formula that stays the same every year. This calculator uses the method most widely reported for recent screening exercises. Compare it with the formula in UNILAG's current post-UTME or screening notice, and if the weights differ, change them under \"Change the formula\" and the result updates.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>How is the UNILAG aggregate score calculated?</h3><p>UNILAG combines three parts: your UTME score converted to 50 marks, your post-UTME screening test scored out of 30, and your five O'level credits worth up to 20 marks. In short: Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME (out of 30) + O'level points (out of 20).</p><h3>Does UNILAG add O'level grades to the aggregate?</h3><p>Yes. O'level grades are worth 20 of the 100 marks, so a better grade in each required subject raises your aggregate. Grades below C6 earn no points and usually mean you don't meet the course requirements.</p><h3>How much does the UNILAG post-UTME score count?</h3><p>The post-UTME is worth 30 of the 100 marks. Each mark you gain in the test adds 1 to your aggregate, against 0.13 for each UTME mark.</p><h2>Other universities</h2><p><a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/ui\">UI aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/oau\">OAU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unn\">UNN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniben\">UNIBEN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniport\">UNIPORT aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilorin\">UNILORIN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/lasu\">LASU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/abu\">ABU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unical\">UNICAL aggregate score calculator</a></p></main>",
 "/aggregate-score-calculator/ui": "<main><h1>UI Aggregate Score Calculator</h1><p>UI gives your UTME score and your post-UTME test equal weight: UTME divided by 8 plus post-UTME divided by 2, for a total out of 100. O'level grades are used to check that you qualify, not to add marks.</p><h2>How UI calculates your aggregate score</h2><p>Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2. The weighting is UTME 50%, post-UTME 50%.</p><ul><li>The UTME part is your JAMB score divided by 8 (out of 50).</li><li>The post-UTME test is scored out of 100 and divided by 2 (out of 50).</li><li>Your O'level result must still meet the course requirements, usually five credits including English Language and Mathematics at not more than two sittings.</li><li>UI has required a UTME score of at least 200 with UI as first choice to apply for screening.</li></ul><h2>UI aggregate score worked example</h2><p>A candidate with 256 in UTME and 67 in the post-UTME test (out of 100) scores:</p><ul><li>UTME: 256 ÷ 8 = 32 (out of 50)</li><li>Post-UTME: 67 ÷ 2 = 33.5 (out of 50)</li><li>Aggregate = 32 + 33.5 = 65.5 out of 100</li></ul><h2>What aggregate score do I need for UI?</h2><p>There is no single pass mark. After screening, UI publishes departmental cut-off marks for each course, and they change from year to year with the number of applicants. Competitive courses such as Medicine, Law, Nursing and Pharmacy always need a much higher aggregate than the general cut-off.</p><p>Enter last year's cut-off for your course as the target in the calculator to see the post-UTME score you would need to reach it.</p><h2>Before you rely on the result</h2><p>The University of Ibadan does not publish one fixed formula that stays the same every year. This calculator uses the method most widely reported for recent screening exercises. Compare it with the formula in UI's current post-UTME or screening notice, and if the weights differ, change them under \"Change the formula\" and the result updates.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>How is the UI aggregate score calculated?</h3><p>UI gives your UTME score and your post-UTME test equal weight: UTME divided by 8 plus post-UTME divided by 2, for a total out of 100. O'level grades are used to check that you qualify, not to add marks. In short: Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2.</p><h3>Does UI add O'level grades to the aggregate?</h3><p>Not in this formula. Your O'level result decides whether you qualify for the course (normally five credits including English Language and Mathematics), but it doesn't add marks to the aggregate.</p><h3>How much does the UI post-UTME score count?</h3><p>The post-UTME is worth 50 of the 100 marks. Each mark you gain in the test adds 0.5 to your aggregate, against 0.13 for each UTME mark.</p><h2>Other universities</h2><p><a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilag\">UNILAG aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/oau\">OAU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unn\">UNN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniben\">UNIBEN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniport\">UNIPORT aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilorin\">UNILORIN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/lasu\">LASU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/abu\">ABU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unical\">UNICAL aggregate score calculator</a></p></main>",
 "/aggregate-score-calculator/oau": "<main><h1>OAU Aggregate Score Calculator</h1><p>OAU is reported to use a 50 : 40 : 10 split: your UTME score is worth 50 marks, the post-UTME test 40 and your O'level grades 10.</p><h2>How OAU calculates your aggregate score</h2><p>Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 100 × 40 + O'level points (out of 10). The weighting is UTME 50%, post-UTME 40%, O'level 10%.</p><ul><li>The UTME part is your JAMB score divided by 8 (out of 50).</li><li>The post-UTME score is scaled to 40 marks. If your result slip shows a score out of 40, set \"out of\" to 40.</li><li>O'level points are A1 = 2, B2 = 1.8, B3 = 1.6, C4 = 1.4, C5 = 1.2 and C6 = 1 across five subjects (10 marks).</li><li>Sources disagree on OAU's method, so compare this with the formula in OAU's current screening notice.</li></ul><h2>OAU aggregate score worked example</h2><p>A candidate with 260 in UTME and 62 in the post-UTME test (out of 100) and O'level grades A1, B3, B2, C4, C5 scores:</p><ul><li>UTME: 260 ÷ 8 = 32.5 (out of 50)</li><li>Post-UTME: 62 ÷ 100 × 40 = 24.8 (out of 40)</li><li>O'level: A1 = 2, B3 = 1.6, B2 = 1.8, C4 = 1.4, C5 = 1.2 → total 8 (out of 10; A1 = 2 per subject)</li><li>Aggregate = 32.5 + 24.8 + 8 = 65.3 out of 100</li></ul><h2>What aggregate score do I need for OAU?</h2><p>There is no single pass mark. After screening, OAU publishes departmental cut-off marks for each course, and they change from year to year with the number of applicants. Competitive courses such as Medicine, Law, Nursing and Pharmacy always need a much higher aggregate than the general cut-off.</p><p>Enter last year's cut-off for your course as the target in the calculator to see the post-UTME score you would need to reach it.</p><h2>Before you rely on the result</h2><p>Obafemi Awolowo University does not publish one fixed formula that stays the same every year. This calculator uses the method most widely reported for recent screening exercises. Compare it with the formula in OAU's current post-UTME or screening notice, and if the weights differ, change them under \"Change the formula\" and the result updates.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>How is the OAU aggregate score calculated?</h3><p>OAU is reported to use a 50 : 40 : 10 split: your UTME score is worth 50 marks, the post-UTME test 40 and your O'level grades 10. In short: Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 100 × 40 + O'level points (out of 10).</p><h3>Does OAU add O'level grades to the aggregate?</h3><p>Yes. O'level grades are worth 10 of the 100 marks, so a better grade in each required subject raises your aggregate. Grades below C6 earn no points and usually mean you don't meet the course requirements.</p><h3>How much does the OAU post-UTME score count?</h3><p>The post-UTME is worth 40 of the 100 marks. Each mark you gain in the test adds 0.4 to your aggregate, against 0.13 for each UTME mark.</p><h2>Other universities</h2><p><a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilag\">UNILAG aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/ui\">UI aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unn\">UNN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniben\">UNIBEN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniport\">UNIPORT aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilorin\">UNILORIN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/lasu\">LASU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/abu\">ABU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unical\">UNICAL aggregate score calculator</a></p></main>",
 "/aggregate-score-calculator/unn": "<main><h1>UNN Aggregate Score Calculator</h1><p>UNN is reported to screen with your UTME and O'level results instead of a written post-UTME test, weighted 60 : 40. UNN usually quotes the result out of 400.</p><h2>How UNN calculates your aggregate score</h2><p>Aggregate = UTME ÷ 400 × 60 + O'level points (out of 40). The weighting is UTME 60%, O'level 40%.</p><ul><li>The UTME part is 60% of the aggregate.</li><li>The O'level part is 40%, from the grades of your relevant subjects: A1 = 100, B2 = 90, B3 = 80, C4 = 70, C5 = 60, C6 = 50 points each (four subjects shown here; change the number if your faculty uses five).</li><li>On the 400 scale this is UTME × 0.6 + O'level points × 0.4; the calculator shows the same result out of 100 and out of 400.</li><li>UNN has sometimes held a written test instead. If yours does, use the custom formula on the main aggregate calculator.</li></ul><h2>UNN aggregate score worked example</h2><p>A candidate with 280 in UTME and O'level grades C5, B3, B2, C4 scores:</p><ul><li>UTME: 280 ÷ 400 × 60 = 42 (out of 60)</li><li>O'level: C5 = 6, B3 = 8, B2 = 9, C4 = 7 → total 30 (out of 40; A1 = 10 per subject)</li><li>Aggregate = 42 + 30 = 72 out of 100</li></ul><p>Out of 400 this is 72 × 4 = 288.</p><h2>What aggregate score do I need for UNN?</h2><p>There is no single pass mark. After screening, UNN publishes departmental cut-off marks for each course, and they change from year to year with the number of applicants. Competitive courses such as Medicine, Law, Nursing and Pharmacy always need a much higher aggregate than the general cut-off.</p><p>Enter last year's cut-off for your course as the target in the calculator to see the UTME score you would need to reach it.</p><h2>Before you rely on the result</h2><p>The University of Nigeria, Nsukka does not publish one fixed formula that stays the same every year. This calculator uses the method most widely reported for recent screening exercises. Compare it with the formula in UNN's current post-UTME or screening notice, and if the weights differ, change them under \"Change the formula\" and the result updates.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>How is the UNN aggregate score calculated?</h3><p>UNN is reported to screen with your UTME and O'level results instead of a written post-UTME test, weighted 60 : 40. UNN usually quotes the result out of 400. In short: Aggregate = UTME ÷ 400 × 60 + O'level points (out of 40).</p><h3>Does UNN add O'level grades to the aggregate?</h3><p>Yes. O'level grades are worth 40 of the 100 marks, so a better grade in each required subject raises your aggregate. Grades below C6 earn no points and usually mean you don't meet the course requirements.</p><h3>Does UNN hold a post-UTME test?</h3><p>UNN has been reported to screen with UTME and O'level results instead of a scored written test. If your year has a test, use the custom formula on the main aggregate calculator page.</p><h2>Other universities</h2><p><a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilag\">UNILAG aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/ui\">UI aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/oau\">OAU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniben\">UNIBEN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniport\">UNIPORT aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilorin\">UNILORIN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/lasu\">LASU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/abu\">ABU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unical\">UNICAL aggregate score calculator</a></p></main>",
 "/aggregate-score-calculator/uniben": "<main><h1>UNIBEN Aggregate Score Calculator</h1><p>UNIBEN is reported to weight the UTME and the post-UTME test equally: UTME divided by 8 plus post-UTME divided by 2, out of 100.</p><h2>How UNIBEN calculates your aggregate score</h2><p>Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2. The weighting is UTME 50%, post-UTME 50%.</p><ul><li>The UTME part is your JAMB score divided by 8 (out of 50).</li><li>The post-UTME is a computer-based test scored out of 100, then divided by 2 (out of 50).</li><li>O'level results decide whether you qualify for the course; they are not added as marks in this formula.</li></ul><h2>UNIBEN aggregate score worked example</h2><p>A candidate with 284 in UTME and 76 in the post-UTME test (out of 100) scores:</p><ul><li>UTME: 284 ÷ 8 = 35.5 (out of 50)</li><li>Post-UTME: 76 ÷ 2 = 38 (out of 50)</li><li>Aggregate = 35.5 + 38 = 73.5 out of 100</li></ul><h2>What aggregate score do I need for UNIBEN?</h2><p>There is no single pass mark. After screening, UNIBEN publishes departmental cut-off marks for each course, and they change from year to year with the number of applicants. Competitive courses such as Medicine, Law, Nursing and Pharmacy always need a much higher aggregate than the general cut-off.</p><p>Enter last year's cut-off for your course as the target in the calculator to see the post-UTME score you would need to reach it.</p><h2>Before you rely on the result</h2><p>The University of Benin does not publish one fixed formula that stays the same every year. This calculator uses the method most widely reported for recent screening exercises. Compare it with the formula in UNIBEN's current post-UTME or screening notice, and if the weights differ, change them under \"Change the formula\" and the result updates.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>How is the UNIBEN aggregate score calculated?</h3><p>UNIBEN is reported to weight the UTME and the post-UTME test equally: UTME divided by 8 plus post-UTME divided by 2, out of 100. In short: Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2.</p><h3>Does UNIBEN add O'level grades to the aggregate?</h3><p>Not in this formula. Your O'level result decides whether you qualify for the course (normally five credits including English Language and Mathematics), but it doesn't add marks to the aggregate.</p><h3>How much does the UNIBEN post-UTME score count?</h3><p>The post-UTME is worth 50 of the 100 marks. Each mark you gain in the test adds 0.5 to your aggregate, against 0.13 for each UTME mark.</p><h2>Other universities</h2><p><a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilag\">UNILAG aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/ui\">UI aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/oau\">OAU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unn\">UNN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniport\">UNIPORT aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilorin\">UNILORIN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/lasu\">LASU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/abu\">ABU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unical\">UNICAL aggregate score calculator</a></p></main>",
 "/aggregate-score-calculator/uniport": "<main><h1>UNIPORT Aggregate Score Calculator</h1><p>UNIPORT is reported to weight the UTME and the post-UTME test equally. Some guides quote the result out of 100 (UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2) and some out of 400; both give the same ranking.</p><h2>How UNIPORT calculates your aggregate score</h2><p>Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2. The weighting is UTME 50%, post-UTME 50%.</p><ul><li>Out of 100: UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2.</li><li>Out of 400: (UTME + post-UTME × 4) ÷ 2, which is exactly 4 times the result out of 100.</li><li>Departmental cut-off marks differ by faculty and change from year to year.</li></ul><h2>UNIPORT aggregate score worked example</h2><p>A candidate with 250 in UTME and 84 in the post-UTME test (out of 100) scores:</p><ul><li>UTME: 250 ÷ 8 = 31.25 (out of 50)</li><li>Post-UTME: 84 ÷ 2 = 42 (out of 50)</li><li>Aggregate = 31.25 + 42 = 73.25 out of 100</li></ul><p>Out of 400 this is 73.25 × 4 = 293.</p><h2>What aggregate score do I need for UNIPORT?</h2><p>There is no single pass mark. After screening, UNIPORT publishes departmental cut-off marks for each course, and they change from year to year with the number of applicants. Competitive courses such as Medicine, Law, Nursing and Pharmacy always need a much higher aggregate than the general cut-off.</p><p>Enter last year's cut-off for your course as the target in the calculator to see the post-UTME score you would need to reach it.</p><h2>Before you rely on the result</h2><p>The University of Port Harcourt does not publish one fixed formula that stays the same every year. This calculator uses the method most widely reported for recent screening exercises. Compare it with the formula in UNIPORT's current post-UTME or screening notice, and if the weights differ, change them under \"Change the formula\" and the result updates.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>How is the UNIPORT aggregate score calculated?</h3><p>UNIPORT is reported to weight the UTME and the post-UTME test equally. Some guides quote the result out of 100 (UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2) and some out of 400; both give the same ranking. In short: Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2.</p><h3>Does UNIPORT add O'level grades to the aggregate?</h3><p>Not in this formula. Your O'level result decides whether you qualify for the course (normally five credits including English Language and Mathematics), but it doesn't add marks to the aggregate.</p><h3>How much does the UNIPORT post-UTME score count?</h3><p>The post-UTME is worth 50 of the 100 marks. Each mark you gain in the test adds 0.5 to your aggregate, against 0.13 for each UTME mark.</p><h2>Other universities</h2><p><a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilag\">UNILAG aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/ui\">UI aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/oau\">OAU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unn\">UNN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniben\">UNIBEN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilorin\">UNILORIN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/lasu\">LASU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/abu\">ABU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unical\">UNICAL aggregate score calculator</a></p></main>",
 "/aggregate-score-calculator/unilorin": "<main><h1>UNILORIN Aggregate Score Calculator</h1><p>UNILORIN is reported to combine three parts: UTME worth 50 marks, the post-UTME test worth 30 and O'level grades worth 20.</p><h2>How UNILORIN calculates your aggregate score</h2><p>Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 100 × 30 + O'level points (out of 20). The weighting is UTME 50%, post-UTME 30%, O'level 20%.</p><ul><li>The UTME part is your JAMB score divided by 8 (out of 50).</li><li>The post-UTME score is scaled to 30 marks. If your result is already out of 30, set \"out of\" to 30.</li><li>O'level points are A1 = 4, B2 = 3.6, B3 = 3.2, C4 = 2.8, C5 = 2.4 and C6 = 2 for five subjects.</li><li>For the 2025 exercise UNILORIN's own bulletin asked for a UTME score of at least 180.</li></ul><h2>UNILORIN aggregate score worked example</h2><p>A candidate with 245 in UTME and 58 in the post-UTME test (out of 100) and O'level grades B2, B3, C4, B3, C5 scores:</p><ul><li>UTME: 245 ÷ 8 = 30.63 (out of 50)</li><li>Post-UTME: 58 ÷ 100 × 30 = 17.4 (out of 30)</li><li>O'level: B2 = 3.6, B3 = 3.2, C4 = 2.8, B3 = 3.2, C5 = 2.4 → total 15.2 (out of 20; A1 = 4 per subject)</li><li>Aggregate = 30.63 + 17.4 + 15.2 = 63.23 out of 100</li></ul><h2>What aggregate score do I need for UNILORIN?</h2><p>There is no single pass mark. After screening, UNILORIN publishes departmental cut-off marks for each course, and they change from year to year with the number of applicants. Competitive courses such as Medicine, Law, Nursing and Pharmacy always need a much higher aggregate than the general cut-off.</p><p>Enter last year's cut-off for your course as the target in the calculator to see the post-UTME score you would need to reach it.</p><h2>Before you rely on the result</h2><p>The University of Ilorin does not publish one fixed formula that stays the same every year. This calculator uses the method most widely reported for recent screening exercises. Compare it with the formula in UNILORIN's current post-UTME or screening notice, and if the weights differ, change them under \"Change the formula\" and the result updates.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>How is the UNILORIN aggregate score calculated?</h3><p>UNILORIN is reported to combine three parts: UTME worth 50 marks, the post-UTME test worth 30 and O'level grades worth 20. In short: Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 100 × 30 + O'level points (out of 20).</p><h3>Does UNILORIN add O'level grades to the aggregate?</h3><p>Yes. O'level grades are worth 20 of the 100 marks, so a better grade in each required subject raises your aggregate. Grades below C6 earn no points and usually mean you don't meet the course requirements.</p><h3>How much does the UNILORIN post-UTME score count?</h3><p>The post-UTME is worth 30 of the 100 marks. Each mark you gain in the test adds 0.3 to your aggregate, against 0.13 for each UTME mark.</p><h2>Other universities</h2><p><a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilag\">UNILAG aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/ui\">UI aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/oau\">OAU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unn\">UNN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniben\">UNIBEN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniport\">UNIPORT aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/lasu\">LASU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/abu\">ABU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unical\">UNICAL aggregate score calculator</a></p></main>",
 "/aggregate-score-calculator/lasu": "<main><h1>LASU Aggregate Score Calculator</h1><p>LASU is reported to use a 50 : 50 split between your UTME score and your O'level grades, with no separate post-UTME test score.</p><h2>How LASU calculates your aggregate score</h2><p>Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + O'level points (out of 50). The weighting is UTME 50%, O'level 50%.</p><ul><li>The UTME part is your JAMB score divided by 8 (out of 50).</li><li>O'level points are A1 = 10, B2 = 9, B3 = 8, C4 = 7, C5 = 6 and C6 = 5 for five subjects (out of 50).</li><li>If LASU holds a written screening test for your year, use the custom formula on the main aggregate calculator.</li></ul><h2>LASU aggregate score worked example</h2><p>A candidate with 275 in UTME and O'level grades A1, B2, B2, B3, C4 scores:</p><ul><li>UTME: 275 ÷ 8 = 34.38 (out of 50)</li><li>O'level: A1 = 10, B2 = 9, B2 = 9, B3 = 8, C4 = 7 → total 43 (out of 50; A1 = 10 per subject)</li><li>Aggregate = 34.38 + 43 = 77.38 out of 100</li></ul><h2>What aggregate score do I need for LASU?</h2><p>There is no single pass mark. After screening, LASU publishes departmental cut-off marks for each course, and they change from year to year with the number of applicants. Competitive courses such as Medicine, Law, Nursing and Pharmacy always need a much higher aggregate than the general cut-off.</p><p>Enter last year's cut-off for your course as the target in the calculator to see the UTME score you would need to reach it.</p><h2>Before you rely on the result</h2><p>Lagos State University does not publish one fixed formula that stays the same every year. This calculator uses the method most widely reported for recent screening exercises. Compare it with the formula in LASU's current post-UTME or screening notice, and if the weights differ, change them under \"Change the formula\" and the result updates.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>How is the LASU aggregate score calculated?</h3><p>LASU is reported to use a 50 : 50 split between your UTME score and your O'level grades, with no separate post-UTME test score. In short: Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + O'level points (out of 50).</p><h3>Does LASU add O'level grades to the aggregate?</h3><p>Yes. O'level grades are worth 50 of the 100 marks, so a better grade in each required subject raises your aggregate. Grades below C6 earn no points and usually mean you don't meet the course requirements.</p><h3>Does LASU hold a post-UTME test?</h3><p>LASU has been reported to screen with UTME and O'level results instead of a scored written test. If your year has a test, use the custom formula on the main aggregate calculator page.</p><h2>Other universities</h2><p><a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilag\">UNILAG aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/ui\">UI aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/oau\">OAU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unn\">UNN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniben\">UNIBEN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniport\">UNIPORT aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilorin\">UNILORIN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/abu\">ABU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unical\">UNICAL aggregate score calculator</a></p></main>",
 "/aggregate-score-calculator/abu": "<main><h1>ABU Aggregate Score Calculator</h1><p>ABU is reported to average your UTME score and your post-UTME score after putting both on a 400 scale: (post-UTME × 4 + UTME) ÷ 2.</p><h2>How ABU calculates your aggregate score</h2><p>Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2. The weighting is UTME 50%, post-UTME 50%.</p><ul><li>Out of 400: (post-UTME × 4 + UTME) ÷ 2. Out of 100 this is UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2.</li><li>ABU also admits by catchment area and merit lists, so the same aggregate can be enough in one list and not in another.</li></ul><h2>ABU aggregate score worked example</h2><p>A candidate with 230 in UTME and 70 in the post-UTME test (out of 100) scores:</p><ul><li>UTME: 230 ÷ 8 = 28.75 (out of 50)</li><li>Post-UTME: 70 ÷ 2 = 35 (out of 50)</li><li>Aggregate = 28.75 + 35 = 63.75 out of 100</li></ul><p>Out of 400 this is 63.75 × 4 = 255.</p><h2>What aggregate score do I need for ABU?</h2><p>There is no single pass mark. After screening, ABU publishes departmental cut-off marks for each course, and they change from year to year with the number of applicants. Competitive courses such as Medicine, Law, Nursing and Pharmacy always need a much higher aggregate than the general cut-off.</p><p>Enter last year's cut-off for your course as the target in the calculator to see the post-UTME score you would need to reach it.</p><h2>Before you rely on the result</h2><p>Ahmadu Bello University, Zaria does not publish one fixed formula that stays the same every year. This calculator uses the method most widely reported for recent screening exercises. Compare it with the formula in ABU's current post-UTME or screening notice, and if the weights differ, change them under \"Change the formula\" and the result updates.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>How is the ABU aggregate score calculated?</h3><p>ABU is reported to average your UTME score and your post-UTME score after putting both on a 400 scale: (post-UTME × 4 + UTME) ÷ 2. In short: Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2.</p><h3>Does ABU add O'level grades to the aggregate?</h3><p>Not in this formula. Your O'level result decides whether you qualify for the course (normally five credits including English Language and Mathematics), but it doesn't add marks to the aggregate.</p><h3>How much does the ABU post-UTME score count?</h3><p>The post-UTME is worth 50 of the 100 marks. Each mark you gain in the test adds 0.5 to your aggregate, against 0.13 for each UTME mark.</p><h2>Other universities</h2><p><a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilag\">UNILAG aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/ui\">UI aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/oau\">OAU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unn\">UNN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniben\">UNIBEN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniport\">UNIPORT aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilorin\">UNILORIN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/lasu\">LASU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unical\">UNICAL aggregate score calculator</a></p></main>",
 "/aggregate-score-calculator/unical": "<main><h1>UNICAL Aggregate Score Calculator</h1><p>UNICAL is reported to average your UTME score and your post-UTME score, so each counts for half of the aggregate.</p><h2>How UNICAL calculates your aggregate score</h2><p>Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2. The weighting is UTME 50%, post-UTME 50%.</p><ul><li>Out of 100: UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2.</li><li>O'level results decide whether you qualify for the course; they are not added as marks in this formula.</li></ul><h2>UNICAL aggregate score worked example</h2><p>A candidate with 240 in UTME and 65 in the post-UTME test (out of 100) scores:</p><ul><li>UTME: 240 ÷ 8 = 30 (out of 50)</li><li>Post-UTME: 65 ÷ 2 = 32.5 (out of 50)</li><li>Aggregate = 30 + 32.5 = 62.5 out of 100</li></ul><h2>What aggregate score do I need for UNICAL?</h2><p>There is no single pass mark. After screening, UNICAL publishes departmental cut-off marks for each course, and they change from year to year with the number of applicants. Competitive courses such as Medicine, Law, Nursing and Pharmacy always need a much higher aggregate than the general cut-off.</p><p>Enter last year's cut-off for your course as the target in the calculator to see the post-UTME score you would need to reach it.</p><h2>Before you rely on the result</h2><p>The University of Calabar does not publish one fixed formula that stays the same every year. This calculator uses the method most widely reported for recent screening exercises. Compare it with the formula in UNICAL's current post-UTME or screening notice, and if the weights differ, change them under \"Change the formula\" and the result updates.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>How is the UNICAL aggregate score calculated?</h3><p>UNICAL is reported to average your UTME score and your post-UTME score, so each counts for half of the aggregate. In short: Aggregate = UTME ÷ 8 + post-UTME ÷ 2.</p><h3>Does UNICAL add O'level grades to the aggregate?</h3><p>Not in this formula. Your O'level result decides whether you qualify for the course (normally five credits including English Language and Mathematics), but it doesn't add marks to the aggregate.</p><h3>How much does the UNICAL post-UTME score count?</h3><p>The post-UTME is worth 50 of the 100 marks. Each mark you gain in the test adds 0.5 to your aggregate, against 0.13 for each UTME mark.</p><h2>Other universities</h2><p><a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilag\">UNILAG aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/ui\">UI aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/oau\">OAU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unn\">UNN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniben\">UNIBEN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/uniport\">UNIPORT aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/unilorin\">UNILORIN aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/lasu\">LASU aggregate score calculator</a> · <a href=\"/aggregate-score-calculator/abu\">ABU aggregate score calculator</a></p></main>",
 "/jamb-cbt-practice/mathematics": "<main><h1>JAMB Mathematics CBT Practice Test</h1><p>Practise UTME Mathematics on a computer-based test that works like the real one: timed questions, a navigator to jump between them, and a full review with the correct answer and an explanation for every question.</p><h2>About these JAMB Mathematics questions</h2><p>There are 45 questions in this mathematics bank, written by StudentToolsNG to the topics in the JAMB UTME Mathematics syllabus and at the level of the exam. They are original practice questions, not copies of JAMB past papers, so you can practise without memorising old answers.</p><p>Each test picks questions at random and shuffles the options, so the answer to a question is not always in the same position. You get 40 seconds a question, the same pace as the UTME, where 180 questions are answered in 2 hours.</p><h2>Topics covered</h2><p>The questions cover 23 topics from the syllabus:</p><ul><li>Number bases (2 questions)</li><li>Fractions and percentages (3 questions)</li><li>Indices (2 questions)</li><li>Logarithms (2 questions)</li><li>Surds (2 questions)</li><li>Sets (1 question)</li><li>Polynomials (2 questions)</li><li>Quadratic equations (1 question)</li><li>Linear equations (1 question)</li><li>Variation (1 question)</li><li>Inequalities (1 question)</li><li>Sequences and series (3 questions)</li><li>Binary operations (1 question)</li><li>Matrices and determinants (2 questions)</li><li>Geometry (2 questions)</li><li>Mensuration (2 questions)</li><li>Trigonometry (3 questions)</li><li>Coordinate geometry (3 questions)</li><li>Differentiation (3 questions)</li><li>Integration (2 questions)</li><li>Statistics (3 questions)</li><li>Permutations and combinations (1 question)</li><li>Probability (2 questions)</li></ul><h2>Tips for JAMB Mathematics</h2><ul><li>Learn the formulas that come up every year: the nth term and sum of an AP and GP, sum to infinity, the remainder theorem, areas and volumes, and the distance and gradient formulas.</li><li>Check an answer by substituting it back. For equations, inequalities and factorisation questions this is often faster than solving from scratch.</li><li>Watch the wording: percentage profit is on the cost price, \"without replacement\" changes the second probability, and order matters for permutations but not combinations.</li><li>Do the short questions first. Flag long statistics or mensuration questions and come back once the quick marks are safe.</li></ul><h2>Sample JAMB Mathematics questions with answers</h2><h3>Question 1: Convert 1011₂ to base ten.</h3><p>A. 11   B. 13   C. 9   D. 22</p><p>Answer: A (11). 1011₂ = 1×2³ + 0×2² + 1×2 + 1 = 8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = 11.</p><h3>Question 2: A trader buys a bag for ₦4,000 and sells it for ₦5,000. What is the percentage profit?</h3><p>A. 20%   B. 25%   C. 10%   D. 30%</p><p>Answer: B (25%). Profit = ₦5,000 − ₦4,000 = ₦1,000. Percentage profit is worked on the cost price: 1,000 ÷ 4,000 × 100 = 25%. (20% is the profit as a share of the selling price, a common mistake.)</p><h3>Question 3: Evaluate log₂8 + log₃9 − log₅1.</h3><p>A. 6   B. 5   C. 4   D. 1</p><p>Answer: B (5). log₂8 = 3 (2³ = 8), log₃9 = 2 (3² = 9) and log₅1 = 0 (any base to power 0 is 1). 3 + 2 − 0 = 5.</p><h3>Question 4: In a class of 40 students, 25 offer Physics, 20 offer Chemistry and 8 offer both. How many offer neither subject?</h3><p>A. 3   B. 5   C. 13   D. 8</p><p>Answer: A (3). n(P ∪ C) = n(P) + n(C) − n(P ∩ C) = 25 + 20 − 8 = 37. Neither = 40 − 37 = 3.</p><h3>Question 5: Solve the simultaneous equations 2x + y = 7 and x − y = 2.</h3><p>A. x = 2, y = 3   B. x = 3, y = 1   C. x = 1, y = 5   D. x = 4, y = −1</p><p>Answer: B (x = 3, y = 1). Add the equations: 3x = 9, so x = 3. Then 3 − y = 2 gives y = 1. (x = 4, y = −1 fits the first equation only.)</p><h3>Question 6: Find the 10th term of the arithmetic progression 3, 7, 11, …</h3><p>A. 43   B. 40   C. 39   D. 35</p><p>Answer: C (39). a = 3, d = 4. Tₙ = a + (n − 1)d = 3 + 9 × 4 = 39.</p><h3>Question 7: Find the determinant of the matrix with rows (3, 2) and (1, 4).</h3><p>A. 14   B. 10   C. −10   D. 11</p><p>Answer: B (10). For rows (a, b) and (c, d) the determinant is ad − bc = 3 × 4 − 2 × 1 = 12 − 2 = 10.</p><h3>Question 8: Find the area of a sector of a circle of radius 7 cm whose angle is 90°. (Take π = 22/7.)</h3><p>A. 154 cm²   B. 77 cm²   C. 38.5 cm²   D. 11 cm²</p><p>Answer: C (38.5 cm²). Area = θ/360 × πr² = 90/360 × 22/7 × 49 = 1/4 × 154 = 38.5 cm².</p><h3>Question 9: From a point on level ground 20 m from the foot of a tower, the angle of elevation of the top is 45°. How tall is the tower?</h3><p>A. 10 m   B. 20√2 m   C. 40 m   D. 20 m</p><p>Answer: D (20 m). tan 45° = height ÷ 20. tan 45° = 1, so the height is 20 m.</p><h3>Question 10: Find the equation of the line with gradient 2 that passes through (1, 3).</h3><p>A. y = 2x + 3   B. y = 2x − 1   C. y = x + 2   D. y = 2x + 1</p><p>Answer: D (y = 2x + 1). y − 3 = 2(x − 1), so y = 2x − 2 + 3 = 2x + 1.</p><h3>Question 11: Find ∫(4x³ + 2x) dx.</h3><p>A. 12x² + 2 + c   B. x⁴ + x² + c   C. 4x⁴ + 2x² + c   D. x⁴ + 2x² + c</p><p>Answer: B (x⁴ + x² + c). Raise each power by one and divide by the new power: 4x⁴/4 + 2x²/2 + c = x⁴ + x² + c. 12x² + 2 is the derivative, not the integral.</p><h3>Question 12: Taking 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 as the whole population, find the variance.</h3><p>A. 2.5   B. 2   C. √2   D. 3</p><p>Answer: B (2). Mean = 3. Squared deviations: 4, 1, 0, 1, 4, total 10. Population variance = 10 ÷ 5 = 2. (Dividing by n − 1 = 4 gives the sample variance 2.5.)</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>Are these JAMB Mathematics past questions?</h3><p>No. They are original questions written to the JAMB Mathematics syllabus in the same style and at the same level as the UTME. Practising on new questions shows whether you understand a topic rather than whether you remember an old answer.</p><h3>How many questions are in the JAMB Mathematics paper?</h3><p>Each of your three other subjects has 40 questions in the UTME, and Use of English has 60, making 180 questions in 2 hours. That is about 40 seconds a question, which is the pace this practice test uses.</p><h3>How is JAMB Mathematics scored?</h3><p>Each subject is scored out of 100 and the four subjects add up to your UTME score out of 400. The percentage you get in this practice test is a rough guide to your score out of 100 in mathematics.</p><h3>Can I practise without a time limit?</h3><p>Yes. Choose Study mode to see the correct answer and the explanation as soon as you pick an option, with no timer. Switch to the timed test once you are ready to practise at exam pace.</p><h2>Other subjects</h2><p><a href=\"/jamb-cbt-practice/physics\">JAMB Physics CBT practice</a> · <a href=\"/jamb-cbt-practice/chemistry\">JAMB Chemistry CBT practice</a> · <a href=\"/jamb-cbt-practice/biology\">JAMB Biology CBT practice</a></p></main>",
 "/jamb-cbt-practice/physics": "<main><h1>JAMB Physics CBT Practice Test</h1><p>Practise UTME Physics on a computer-based test that works like the real one: timed questions, a navigator to jump between them, and a full review with the correct answer and an explanation for every question.</p><h2>About these JAMB Physics questions</h2><p>There are 49 questions in this physics bank, written by StudentToolsNG to the topics in the JAMB UTME Physics syllabus and at the level of the exam. They are original practice questions, not copies of JAMB past papers, so you can practise without memorising old answers.</p><p>Each test picks questions at random and shuffles the options, so the answer to a question is not always in the same position. You get 40 seconds a question, the same pace as the UTME, where 180 questions are answered in 2 hours.</p><h2>Topics covered</h2><p>The questions cover 32 topics from the syllabus:</p><ul><li>Measurement and units (2 questions)</li><li>Scalars and vectors (2 questions)</li><li>Motion (4 questions)</li><li>Newton's laws and momentum (3 questions)</li><li>Circular motion (1 question)</li><li>Gravitational field (1 question)</li><li>Equilibrium of forces (1 question)</li><li>Work, energy and power (2 questions)</li><li>Simple machines (1 question)</li><li>Friction (1 question)</li><li>Elasticity (1 question)</li><li>Pressure (1 question)</li><li>Liquids at rest (1 question)</li><li>Temperature (1 question)</li><li>Heat energy (2 questions)</li><li>Gas laws (2 questions)</li><li>Thermal expansion (1 question)</li><li>Heat transfer (1 question)</li><li>Waves (1 question)</li><li>Sound (2 questions)</li><li>Reflection of light (1 question)</li><li>Refraction of light (2 questions)</li><li>Lenses (2 questions)</li><li>Electrostatics (1 question)</li><li>Capacitors (2 questions)</li><li>Current electricity (3 questions)</li><li>Magnetism (1 question)</li><li>Electromagnetic induction (1 question)</li><li>Simple a.c. circuits (1 question)</li><li>Radioactivity (2 questions)</li><li>Modern physics (1 question)</li><li>Electronics (1 question)</li></ul><h2>Tips for JAMB Physics</h2><ul><li>Use g = 10 m/s² unless the question gives another value, and convert to SI units (cm to m, μF to F, °C to K for gas laws) before you substitute.</li><li>Most calculation questions are a single formula. Write down the formula first, then put in the numbers, so you don't mix up similar ones like Q = CV and E = ½CV².</li><li>Inverse-square laws (gravitation, Coulomb's law, intensity) and ratios (transformers, Hooke's law, gas laws) can often be answered without a calculator.</li><li>Learn the definitions and units: many questions only ask which quantity is a vector, what an alpha particle is, or which unit goes with which quantity.</li></ul><h2>Sample JAMB Physics questions with answers</h2><h3>Question 1: What is the SI unit of power?</h3><p>A. Joule   B. Watt   C. Newton   D. Pascal</p><p>Answer: B (Watt). Power is the rate of doing work, measured in joules per second, which is the watt (W). The joule is the unit of energy, the newton of force and the pascal of pressure.</p><h3>Question 2: A car starts from rest and accelerates uniformly to 20 m/s in 5 s. How far does it travel in this time?</h3><p>A. 100 m   B. 4 m   C. 50 m   D. 25 m</p><p>Answer: C (50 m). Distance = average velocity × time = (0 + 20)/2 × 5 = 50 m. (The acceleration is 4 m/s², and s = ½at² = ½ × 4 × 25 = 50 m too.)</p><h3>Question 3: What force gives a 5 kg mass an acceleration of 4 m/s²?</h3><p>A. 1.25 N   B. 9 N   C. 20 N   D. 0.8 N</p><p>Answer: C (20 N). Newton's second law: F = ma = 5 × 4 = 20 N. Dividing (1.25 N or 0.8 N) or adding (9 N) the numbers are the usual slips.</p><h3>Question 4: If the distance between two masses is doubled, the gravitational force between them becomes</h3><p>A. half as large   B. one quarter as large   C. twice as large   D. four times as large</p><p>Answer: B (one quarter as large). Newton's law of gravitation is an inverse-square law, F ∝ 1/r². Doubling r divides F by 2² = 4.</p><h3>Question 5: A machine has a mechanical advantage of 4 and a velocity ratio of 5. What is its efficiency?</h3><p>A. 80%   B. 125%   C. 20%   D. 90%</p><p>Answer: A (80%). Efficiency = MA ÷ VR × 100% = 4/5 × 100% = 80%. Efficiency can never be over 100%.</p><h3>Question 6: A solid weighs 10 N in air and 6 N when fully immersed in water. What is its relative density?</h3><p>A. 1.67   B. 0.4   C. 2.5   D. 4</p><p>Answer: C (2.5). Upthrust = 10 − 6 = 4 N, the weight of water displaced. Relative density = weight in air ÷ upthrust = 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5.</p><h3>Question 7: A gas occupies 300 cm³ at 27 °C. It is heated at constant pressure to 127 °C. What is its new volume?</h3><p>A. 1,411 cm³   B. 225 cm³   C. 300 cm³   D. 400 cm³</p><p>Answer: D (400 cm³). Charles's law uses kelvin: V₂ = V₁ × T₂/T₁ = 300 × 400/300 = 400 cm³. Using °C (300 × 127/27) gives the wrong answer 1,411 cm³.</p><h3>Question 8: A wave has a frequency of 50 Hz and a wavelength of 4 m. What is its speed?</h3><p>A. 12.5 m/s   B. 200 m/s   C. 0.08 m/s   D. 54 m/s</p><p>Answer: B (200 m/s). The wave equation: v = fλ = 50 × 4 = 200 m/s. Every wave, sound or light, obeys v = fλ.</p><h3>Question 9: A ray of light passes from air into glass. The angle of incidence is 60° and the angle of refraction is 30°. What is the refractive index of the glass?</h3><p>A. 2.00   B. 0.58   C. 1.73   D. 1.50</p><p>Answer: C (1.73). Snell's law: n = sin i ÷ sin r = sin 60° ÷ sin 30° = 0.866 ÷ 0.5 = 1.73 (that is √3).</p><h3>Question 10: Two point charges repel each other with a force F. If the distance between them is halved, the force becomes</h3><p>A. 2F   B. F/2   C. 4F   D. F/4</p><p>Answer: C (4F). Coulomb's law: F ∝ 1/r². Halving r multiplies the force by 2² = 4.</p><h3>Question 11: A cell of e.m.f. 3 V and internal resistance 1 Ω is connected to a 5 Ω resistor. What is the terminal potential difference?</h3><p>A. 3.0 V   B. 0.5 V   C. 2.5 V   D. 0.6 V</p><p>Answer: C (2.5 V). I = E ÷ (R + r) = 3 ÷ 6 = 0.5 A. Terminal p.d. V = IR = 0.5 × 5 = 2.5 V; the other 0.5 V is lost inside the cell.</p><h3>Question 12: An alternating voltage has a peak value of 100 V. What is its r.m.s. value?</h3><p>A. 141.4 V   B. 70.7 V   C. 50 V   D. 100 V</p><p>Answer: B (70.7 V). Vrms = Vpeak ÷ √2 = 100 ÷ 1.414 = 70.7 V.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>Are these JAMB Physics past questions?</h3><p>No. They are original questions written to the JAMB Physics syllabus in the same style and at the same level as the UTME. Practising on new questions shows whether you understand a topic rather than whether you remember an old answer.</p><h3>How many questions are in the JAMB Physics paper?</h3><p>Each of your three other subjects has 40 questions in the UTME, and Use of English has 60, making 180 questions in 2 hours. That is about 40 seconds a question, which is the pace this practice test uses.</p><h3>How is JAMB Physics scored?</h3><p>Each subject is scored out of 100 and the four subjects add up to your UTME score out of 400. The percentage you get in this practice test is a rough guide to your score out of 100 in physics.</p><h3>Can I practise without a time limit?</h3><p>Yes. Choose Study mode to see the correct answer and the explanation as soon as you pick an option, with no timer. Switch to the timed test once you are ready to practise at exam pace.</p><h2>Other subjects</h2><p><a href=\"/jamb-cbt-practice/mathematics\">JAMB Mathematics CBT practice</a> · <a href=\"/jamb-cbt-practice/chemistry\">JAMB Chemistry CBT practice</a> · <a href=\"/jamb-cbt-practice/biology\">JAMB Biology CBT practice</a></p></main>",
 "/jamb-cbt-practice/chemistry": "<main><h1>JAMB Chemistry CBT Practice Test</h1><p>Practise UTME Chemistry on a computer-based test that works like the real one: timed questions, a navigator to jump between them, and a full review with the correct answer and an explanation for every question.</p><h2>About these JAMB Chemistry questions</h2><p>There are 45 questions in this chemistry bank, written by StudentToolsNG to the topics in the JAMB UTME Chemistry syllabus and at the level of the exam. They are original practice questions, not copies of JAMB past papers, so you can practise without memorising old answers.</p><p>Each test picks questions at random and shuffles the options, so the answer to a question is not always in the same position. You get 40 seconds a question, the same pace as the UTME, where 180 questions are answered in 2 hours.</p><h2>Topics covered</h2><p>The questions cover 20 topics from the syllabus:</p><ul><li>Separation techniques (2 questions)</li><li>The mole concept (3 questions)</li><li>Chemical formulae and equations (2 questions)</li><li>Atomic structure (3 questions)</li><li>Periodic table (2 questions)</li><li>Chemical bonding (3 questions)</li><li>Gas laws and kinetic theory (2 questions)</li><li>Air and water (2 questions)</li><li>Solubility (1 question)</li><li>Environmental pollution (1 question)</li><li>Acids, bases and salts (3 questions)</li><li>Oxidation and reduction (2 questions)</li><li>Electrolysis (2 questions)</li><li>Energy changes (1 question)</li><li>Rates of reaction (1 question)</li><li>Chemical equilibrium (2 questions)</li><li>Non-metals and their compounds (3 questions)</li><li>Metals and their compounds (2 questions)</li><li>Organic chemistry (5 questions)</li><li>Chemistry and industry (3 questions)</li></ul><h2>Tips for JAMB Chemistry</h2><ul><li>Get comfortable with the IUPAC names the UTME uses, such as tetraoxosulfate(VI) for sulfate and trioxocarbonate(IV) for carbonate.</li><li>Mole calculations are the backbone of the paper: moles = mass ÷ molar mass, 22.4 dm³ per mole at s.t.p., and always use the mole ratio from the balanced equation.</li><li>For equilibrium questions, apply Le Chatelier's principle: count the gas moles on each side for pressure and check the sign of ΔH for temperature.</li><li>Memorise the tests for gases and ions (lime water, litmus, glowing splint, flame colours); they are quick marks.</li></ul><h2>Sample JAMB Chemistry questions with answers</h2><h3>Question 1: Which method is best for separating a mixture of ethanol and water?</h3><p>A. Filtration   B. Fractional distillation   C. Evaporation   D. Decantation</p><p>Answer: B (Fractional distillation). Ethanol and water mix completely but boil at different temperatures (about 78 °C and 100 °C), so fractional distillation separates them.</p><h3>Question 2: What volume does 0.5 mol of a gas occupy at s.t.p.?</h3><p>A. 22.4 dm³   B. 11.2 dm³   C. 44.8 dm³   D. 5.6 dm³</p><p>Answer: B (11.2 dm³). One mole of any gas occupies 22.4 dm³ at s.t.p., so 0.5 mol occupies 0.5 × 22.4 = 11.2 dm³.</p><h3>Question 3: An atom has mass number 23 and atomic number 11. How many neutrons does it have?</h3><p>A. 11   B. 12   C. 23   D. 34</p><p>Answer: B (12). Neutrons = mass number − atomic number = 23 − 11 = 12. (This is sodium.)</p><h3>Question 4: Elements in group 1 of the periodic table are known as the</h3><p>A. halogens   B. noble gases   C. alkaline earth metals   D. alkali metals</p><p>Answer: D (alkali metals). Group 1 (lithium, sodium, potassium…) are the alkali metals. Group 2 are the alkaline earth metals, group 7 the halogens and group 0 the noble gases.</p><h3>Question 5: Under the same conditions, how many times faster than oxygen does hydrogen diffuse? (H₂ = 2, O₂ = 32)</h3><p>A. 16   B. 2   C. 4   D. 8</p><p>Answer: C (4). Graham's law: rate ∝ 1/√M. Rate(H₂) ÷ rate(O₂) = √(32/2) = √16 = 4.</p><h3>Question 6: Temporary hardness of water is caused by dissolved</h3><p>A. calcium tetraoxosulfate(VI)   B. sodium chloride   C. calcium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV)   D. magnesium tetraoxosulfate(VI)</p><p>Answer: C (calcium hydrogentrioxocarbonate(IV)). Calcium hydrogencarbonate, Ca(HCO₃)₂, breaks down on boiling to insoluble CaCO₃, so the hardness can be removed by boiling. Sulfates cause permanent hardness.</p><h3>Question 7: What volume of 0.1 mol/dm³ H₂SO₄ neutralises 25 cm³ of 0.1 mol/dm³ NaOH? H₂SO₄ + 2NaOH → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O</h3><p>A. 25 cm³   B. 12.5 cm³   C. 50 cm³   D. 6.25 cm³</p><p>Answer: B (12.5 cm³). Moles of NaOH = 0.1 × 25/1,000 = 0.0025. The equation needs half as much acid: 0.00125 mol. Volume = 0.00125 ÷ 0.1 = 0.0125 dm³ = 12.5 cm³.</p><h3>Question 8: What mass of copper is deposited when 19,300 C of charge passes through copper(II) tetraoxosulfate(VI) solution? (Cu = 64, F = 96,500 C/mol)</h3><p>A. 12.8 g   B. 3.2 g   C. 64 g   D. 6.4 g</p><p>Answer: D (6.4 g). Moles of electrons = 19,300 ÷ 96,500 = 0.2. Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so 0.2 mol of electrons deposits 0.1 mol of copper = 0.1 × 64 = 6.4 g.</p><h3>Question 9: For N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), ΔH negative, what is the effect of increasing the pressure?</h3><p>A. The equilibrium shifts to the left   B. There is no effect   C. The equilibrium shifts to the right   D. Only the rate of the backward reaction rises</p><p>Answer: C (The equilibrium shifts to the right). There are 4 moles of gas on the left and 2 on the right. By Le Chatelier's principle, higher pressure favours the side with fewer gas moles, so more ammonia forms.</p><h3>Question 10: Which gas turns damp red litmus paper blue?</h3><p>A. Chlorine   B. Ammonia   C. Hydrogen chloride   D. Sulfur(IV) oxide</p><p>Answer: B (Ammonia). Ammonia is the common alkaline gas. Chlorine bleaches litmus, while hydrogen chloride and sulfur(IV) oxide are acidic and turn blue litmus red.</p><h3>Question 11: What is the general formula of the alkanes?</h3><p>A. CₙH₂ₙ   B. CₙH₂ₙ₊₂   C. CₙH₂ₙ₋₂   D. CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH</p><p>Answer: B (CₙH₂ₙ₊₂). Alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons, CₙH₂ₙ₊₂ (methane CH₄, ethane C₂H₆). CₙH₂ₙ is the alkenes, CₙH₂ₙ₋₂ the alkynes and CₙH₂ₙ₊₁OH the alkanols.</p><h3>Question 12: How many structural isomers does butane, C₄H₁₀, have?</h3><p>A. 1   B. 3   C. 2   D. 4</p><p>Answer: C (2). Two: the straight chain (butane) and the branched chain (2-methylpropane).</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>Are these JAMB Chemistry past questions?</h3><p>No. They are original questions written to the JAMB Chemistry syllabus in the same style and at the same level as the UTME. Practising on new questions shows whether you understand a topic rather than whether you remember an old answer.</p><h3>How many questions are in the JAMB Chemistry paper?</h3><p>Each of your three other subjects has 40 questions in the UTME, and Use of English has 60, making 180 questions in 2 hours. That is about 40 seconds a question, which is the pace this practice test uses.</p><h3>How is JAMB Chemistry scored?</h3><p>Each subject is scored out of 100 and the four subjects add up to your UTME score out of 400. The percentage you get in this practice test is a rough guide to your score out of 100 in chemistry.</p><h3>Can I practise without a time limit?</h3><p>Yes. Choose Study mode to see the correct answer and the explanation as soon as you pick an option, with no timer. Switch to the timed test once you are ready to practise at exam pace.</p><h2>Other subjects</h2><p><a href=\"/jamb-cbt-practice/mathematics\">JAMB Mathematics CBT practice</a> · <a href=\"/jamb-cbt-practice/physics\">JAMB Physics CBT practice</a> · <a href=\"/jamb-cbt-practice/biology\">JAMB Biology CBT practice</a></p></main>",
 "/jamb-cbt-practice/biology": "<main><h1>JAMB Biology CBT Practice Test</h1><p>Practise UTME Biology on a computer-based test that works like the real one: timed questions, a navigator to jump between them, and a full review with the correct answer and an explanation for every question.</p><h2>About these JAMB Biology questions</h2><p>There are 45 questions in this biology bank, written by StudentToolsNG to the topics in the JAMB UTME Biology syllabus and at the level of the exam. They are original practice questions, not copies of JAMB past papers, so you can practise without memorising old answers.</p><p>Each test picks questions at random and shuffles the options, so the answer to a question is not always in the same position. You get 40 seconds a question, the same pace as the UTME, where 180 questions are answered in 2 hours.</p><h2>Topics covered</h2><p>The questions cover 14 topics from the syllabus:</p><ul><li>The cell (3 questions)</li><li>Cell and its environment (2 questions)</li><li>Classification (3 questions)</li><li>Variety of organisms (2 questions)</li><li>Nutrition (5 questions)</li><li>Transport (4 questions)</li><li>Respiration (2 questions)</li><li>Excretion (2 questions)</li><li>Support and movement (2 questions)</li><li>Reproduction (3 questions)</li><li>Co-ordination (5 questions)</li><li>Ecology (6 questions)</li><li>Heredity and variation (4 questions)</li><li>Evolution (2 questions)</li></ul><h2>Tips for JAMB Biology</h2><ul><li>Read every option. Biology questions often hinge on one word, such as the pulmonary vein (oxygenated) versus the pulmonary artery (deoxygenated).</li><li>Learn the classic pairs that get swapped: tendon and ligament, xylem and phloem, rods and cones, insulin and glucagon, mitosis and meiosis.</li><li>Practise genetic crosses until they are automatic. Monohybrid ratios (3 : 1 and 1 : 2 : 1) and AS × AS crosses come up every year.</li><li>Ecology carries a lot of marks: food chains and trophic levels, the nitrogen and carbon cycles, sampling tools and Nigeria's vegetation zones.</li></ul><h2>Sample JAMB Biology questions with answers</h2><h3>Question 1: Which organelle is the main site of aerobic respiration?</h3><p>A. Ribosome   B. Mitochondrion   C. Nucleus   D. Golgi apparatus</p><p>Answer: B (Mitochondrion). Aerobic respiration releases energy (as ATP) in the mitochondria. Ribosomes make proteins, and the nucleus holds the genetic material.</p><h3>Question 2: Osmosis is the movement of</h3><p>A. water molecules from a dilute to a more concentrated solution through a selectively permeable membrane   B. solute molecules from a concentrated to a dilute solution   C. water against a concentration gradient, using energy   D. any molecule from high to low concentration without a membrane</p><p>Answer: A (water molecules from a dilute to a more concentrated solution through a selectively permeable membrane). Osmosis is a special case of diffusion: water moves across a selectively permeable membrane from a region of high water concentration (dilute) to low (concentrated).</p><h3>Question 3: In the scientific name Homo sapiens, the word Homo is the</h3><p>A. species   B. family   C. genus   D. order</p><p>Answer: C (genus). Binomial nomenclature gives the genus first (with a capital letter) and the species second.</p><h3>Question 4: A food sample turns blue-black when iodine solution is added. The food contains</h3><p>A. protein   B. starch   C. reducing sugar   D. fat</p><p>Answer: B (starch). Iodine turns blue-black with starch. Protein gives purple with Biuret reagent, reducing sugar gives brick-red with Benedict's solution and fat leaves a translucent spot on paper.</p><h3>Question 5: Which blood vessel carries oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart?</h3><p>A. Pulmonary artery   B. Vena cava   C. Pulmonary vein   D. Aorta</p><p>Answer: C (Pulmonary vein). The pulmonary vein is the only vein that carries oxygenated blood. The pulmonary artery is the only artery that carries deoxygenated blood (from the heart to the lungs).</p><h3>Question 6: Which conditions increase the rate of transpiration?</h3><p>A. High humidity and still air   B. Darkness   C. Cold, still air   D. Warm, dry and windy air</p><p>Answer: D (Warm, dry and windy air). Warmth speeds evaporation, dry air keeps the water-vapour gradient steep and wind removes moist air from the leaf surface. In darkness most stomata close.</p><h3>Question 7: In mammals, urea is made in the</h3><p>A. kidney   B. bladder   C. liver   D. pancreas</p><p>Answer: C (liver). The liver breaks down excess amino acids (deamination) and turns the ammonia into urea. The kidneys only remove urea from the blood.</p><h3>Question 8: In humans, fertilisation normally takes place in the</h3><p>A. uterus   B. ovary   C. vagina   D. oviduct</p><p>Answer: D (oviduct). The sperm meets the egg in the oviduct (fallopian tube). The fertilised egg then moves to the uterus, where it implants.</p><h3>Question 9: In a reflex arc, which is the correct order?</h3><p>A. Receptor → sensory neurone → relay neurone → motor neurone → effector   B. Receptor → motor neurone → relay neurone → sensory neurone → effector   C. Effector → sensory neurone → relay neurone → motor neurone → receptor   D. Receptor → relay neurone → sensory neurone → motor neurone → effector</p><p>Answer: A (Receptor → sensory neurone → relay neurone → motor neurone → effector). The receptor detects the stimulus, the sensory neurone carries the impulse to the spinal cord, the relay neurone passes it on, and the motor neurone carries it to the effector (a muscle or gland).</p><h3>Question 10: In the food chain grass → grasshopper → frog → snake, the frog is a</h3><p>A. producer   B. primary consumer   C. secondary consumer   D. tertiary consumer</p><p>Answer: C (secondary consumer). Grass is the producer, the grasshopper (eats grass) is the primary consumer, the frog (eats the grasshopper) is the secondary consumer and the snake is the tertiary consumer.</p><h3>Question 11: Which instrument is used to estimate the population of plants in an area?</h3><p>A. Pooter   B. Sweep net   C. Quadrat   D. Tullgren funnel</p><p>Answer: C (Quadrat). A quadrat (usually a 1 m square frame) is placed at random and the plants inside are counted. A pooter collects small insects and a Tullgren funnel extracts soil organisms.</p><h3>Question 12: In humans, the sex of a child is determined by</h3><p>A. the mother's egg   B. the father's sperm   C. both parents equally   D. the environment</p><p>Answer: B (the father's sperm). Every egg carries an X chromosome. A sperm carries either X (giving a girl, XX) or Y (giving a boy, XY), so the father's sperm decides.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>Are these JAMB Biology past questions?</h3><p>No. They are original questions written to the JAMB Biology syllabus in the same style and at the same level as the UTME. Practising on new questions shows whether you understand a topic rather than whether you remember an old answer.</p><h3>How many questions are in the JAMB Biology paper?</h3><p>Each of your three other subjects has 40 questions in the UTME, and Use of English has 60, making 180 questions in 2 hours. That is about 40 seconds a question, which is the pace this practice test uses.</p><h3>How is JAMB Biology scored?</h3><p>Each subject is scored out of 100 and the four subjects add up to your UTME score out of 400. The percentage you get in this practice test is a rough guide to your score out of 100 in biology.</p><h3>Can I practise without a time limit?</h3><p>Yes. Choose Study mode to see the correct answer and the explanation as soon as you pick an option, with no timer. Switch to the timed test once you are ready to practise at exam pace.</p><h2>Other subjects</h2><p><a href=\"/jamb-cbt-practice/mathematics\">JAMB Mathematics CBT practice</a> · <a href=\"/jamb-cbt-practice/physics\">JAMB Physics CBT practice</a> · <a href=\"/jamb-cbt-practice/chemistry\">JAMB Chemistry CBT practice</a></p></main>"
};
